globalThis.DominanceCellContent={"version":1,"status":"academic-review-pending","course":"Cell Structure","scope":"HB1:First","lessons":[{"id":"lesson_01","title":"Lesson 1 — Why Microscopy Matters","markdown":"# Lesson 1 — Why Microscopy Matters\n\n### Microscopy and the limits of the eye\nThe unaided human eye can distinguish objects approximately **0.1 mm (100 µm)** apart under suitable conditions. Many cells and their internal structures are smaller than this, so microscopes are needed to study them. Cells may also be nearly transparent, making **contrast** important for seeing their boundaries and components.\n\nThree ideas are essential:\n- **Magnification** makes an object appear larger.\n- **Resolution (resolving power)** is the ability to distinguish two closely spaced points as separate.\n- **Contrast** is the difference in appearance between a structure and its surroundings, making it easier to see.\n\nA highly magnified image is not necessarily a clear image: magnification without adequate resolution may only enlarge blur.\n\n### Units used in microscopy\n| Unit | Symbol | Metres | Useful relationship |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Millimetre | mm | 10⁻³ m | 1 mm = 1,000 µm |\n| Micrometre (micron) | µm | 10⁻⁶ m | 1 µm = 1,000 nm |\n| Nanometre | nm | 10⁻⁹ m | 1 nm = 10 Å |\n| Angstrom | Å | 10⁻¹⁰ m | 10 Å = 1 nm |\n\n**Older notation:** **mµ** (millimicron) is an older name for **nm**. Thus 1,000 mµ = 1 µm and 10⁶ mµ = 1 mm.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- Approximate unaided-eye resolving limit: **0.1 mm = 100 µm**.\n- Microscopy requires high **resolving power** and sufficient **contrast**, especially for transparent cells.\n- **1 mm = 1,000 µm = 10⁶ nm**; **1 µm = 1,000 nm**; **1 nm = 10 Å**.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Small cell structures → appropriate units → microscopy:** Cell dimensions are commonly expressed in micrometres; much smaller structures may be expressed in nanometres. **Magnification + resolution + contrast → useful image:** All three contribute to what can actually be seen. Later lessons explain how microscope lenses affect image quality and resolution.\n\n**Optional check:** Convert 0.1 mm into micrometres. **Answer:** 100 µm.\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_02","title":"Lesson 2 — Simple Microscopes","markdown":"# Lesson 2 — Simple Microscopes\n\n### Types of light microscopes\nThe two basic types introduced here are **simple microscopes** and **compound microscopes**.\n\nA **simple microscope** uses **one magnifying lens**. Familiar examples are a **magnifying glass** and a **simple single-lens loupe**. A **compound microscope** uses more than one lens system.\n\n### Image formation\nA convex (converging) lens can produce an **upright, enlarged virtual image** when the object is placed closer to the lens than its focal point. A *virtual image* appears to be located where light rays seem to come from; the rays do not actually meet at that apparent image position.\n\n\n### Simple microscopes in use\nModern simple microscopes are often handheld and useful for fieldwork or quick examination of objects. Older designs could be mounted in stands resembling those of more elaborate microscopes, but they still used a single magnifying lens.\n\nA **loupe** is a compact magnifier for examining small details. Unlike a typical handheld magnifying glass, it usually has no attached handle; its lens or lenses may sit in an opaque cylindrical or conical housing or fold into a protective case.\n\n**Understanding note:** Some modern loupes contain multiple lens elements. A loupe is therefore not automatically a *single-lens* microscope simply because it is compact.\n\n### Early contributors\n- **Robert Hooke (1635–1703)** — an early figure in microscopy, associated with observations of cork cells.\n- **Antony van Leeuwenhoek (1632–1723)** — known for using powerful single-lens microscopes to observe microorganisms.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- **Two types of light microscopes:** simple and compound.\n- **Simple microscope:** one lens; examples include magnifying glasses and simple loupes.\n- **Modern simple microscopes:** often handheld, suitable for fieldwork and quick viewing.\n- **Loupe:** small magnifier, generally without a handle; lens housing may be cylindrical, conical or folding.\n- **Hooke:** 1635–1703; **van Leeuwenhoek:** 1632–1723.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Convex lens → magnified virtual image:** A converging lens can enlarge the apparent size of nearby objects. **Simple versus compound microscope:** The distinction concerns the lens arrangement, not merely the instrument's size. **Lens design → image defects:** Even when magnification is achieved, imperfections in lenses may blur or colour the image; these are examined next.\n\n**Optional check:** What distinguishes a simple microscope from a compound microscope? **Answer:** A simple microscope uses one magnifying lens; a compound microscope uses multiple lens systems.\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_03","title":"Lesson 3 — Optical Aberrations","markdown":"# Lesson 3 — Optical Aberrations\n\n### What are optical aberrations?\n**Optical aberrations** are lens defects that reduce image quality. Two major defects of simple microscopes are **spherical aberration** and **chromatic aberration**.\n\n### Spherical aberration\n**Spherical aberration** produces blurred images because light rays from a specimen do not all come to the same focus. In a spherical lens, rays near the edge (*peripheral rays*) and rays near the centre are refracted differently, so they may focus at different points. This reduces sharpness and can make fine details harder to distinguish.\n\nDifferences in refractive index between materials along the light path, such as glass and air, can also contribute to focusing problems. **Refractive index** describes how strongly light is slowed and bent by a material.\n\n**Ways to reduce spherical aberration:**\n1. **Coat or block the periphery of the lens with opaque material** to restrict peripheral rays.\n2. **Use suitable lens shapes, including plano-convex lenses** (one flat surface and one curved surface).\n3. **Use an oil-immersion lens** to reduce changes in refraction between the specimen and the objective lens.\n\nAn **aspherical lens** (with a specially shaped, non-spherical surface) is another method of bringing rays closer to a common focus.\n\n**Understanding note — immersion oil:** The **objective lens** is the microscope lens nearest the specimen. In a typical covered slide, immersion oil fills the gap between the objective lens and the coverslip. Because the oil bends light similarly to glass, it helps reduce unwanted refraction. It does **not** guarantee complete elimination of every cause of spherical aberration.\n\n**Understanding note — lens shape:** A plano-convex lens can reduce spherical aberration in suitable arrangements; it does not automatically remove the defect in every setup.\n\n### Chromatic aberration\n**Chromatic aberration**, also called **colour fringing** or **purple fringing**, occurs when a lens focuses different wavelengths (colours) of light at different positions. It results from **dispersion**: the refractive index of glass varies with wavelength.\n\nThe image may become blurred or develop coloured edges, including red, green, blue, yellow, purple or magenta fringes.\n\n**Ways to correct chromatic aberration:**\n1. **Combine crown glass and flint glass in an achromatic doublet.** Their different optical properties help bring selected colours to approximately the same focus.\n2. **Use more complex combinations of lenses** to improve correction.\n\n**Understanding note:** The slide describes crown and flint glass as having equal but opposite focal lengths. In practice, an achromatic doublet is designed by balancing the optical powers and dispersions of its elements; the simple wording should not be taken as a universal construction rule.\n\n### Spherical versus chromatic aberration\n| | Spherical aberration | Chromatic aberration |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main issue | Rays passing through different lens regions focus differently | Different colours focus differently |\n| Visible effect | Blur and reduced sharpness | Blur and coloured fringes |\n| Typical remedies | Restrict peripheral rays; suitable lens shapes; immersion techniques | Achromatic doublets; more complex corrected lens systems |\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- **Two inherent optical defects:** spherical and chromatic aberration.\n- **Spherical:** rays fail to meet at a simultaneous focus; image becomes blurred.\n- **Spherical correction list:** opaque peripheral coating; plano-convex lenses; oil immersion. Also understand the role of aspherical lenses.\n- **Chromatic:** dispersion causes different colours to focus differently; also called colour/purple fringing.\n- **Chromatic correction list:** crown + flint glass (**achromatic doublet**); more complex lens systems.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Refraction → focusing:** Lens surfaces bend light to form images. **Lens region → spherical aberration:** Central and peripheral rays may focus differently. **Wavelength → dispersion → chromatic aberration:** Different colours may focus at different positions. **Corrected lenses → clearer microscopy:** Lens design affects how much detail remains visible. **Immersion oil → refractive-index continuity:** A properly matched oil reduces unwanted bending at the glass–oil interface, particularly with a compatible objective lens.\n\n**Optional checks:**\n1. Which defect causes coloured fringes? **Chromatic aberration.**\n2. Name the two glasses in an achromatic doublet. **Crown and flint glass.**\n3. State the three spherical-aberration correction methods listed above. **Opaque peripheral coating, suitable plano-convex lenses, and oil immersion.**\n\n---\n\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_04","title":"Lesson 4 — Compound and Dissecting Microscopes","markdown":"# Lesson 4 — Compound and Dissecting Microscopes\n\n## Light microscopy\n**Light microscopy** uses visible light to observe specimens. Lens curvature and lens material are selected to reduce distortion of image shapes and colours.\n\n## Two types of light microscopes\n\n**1. Compound microscope.** A modern compound light microscope uses a series of lenses and visible light to magnify a specimen. For transmitted-light viewing, specimens must be thin enough to allow light through them. The instrument typically includes an **eyepiece (ocular lens)**, **objective lenses** (the lenses closest to the specimen), a stage, illumination and focusing controls.\n\n**2. Dissecting microscope.** A dissecting microscope permits viewing of **opaque specimens**—objects that do not allow light to pass through them. It is useful for observing larger specimens and their external features.\n\n**Monocular** means one eyepiece; **binocular** means two eyepieces. These describe the viewing arrangement, not whether the instrument is compound or dissecting.\n\n## Eyepiece or ocular lens\nThe **eyepiece** is the magnifying lens at the top of a compound microscope that the observer looks through. Common eyepiece magnifications are **10× and 15×**. The **objective lens** is positioned nearest the specimen and provides the first stage of magnification.\n\n**Understanding note:** In a compound microscope, both the objective and eyepiece contribute to the final magnification; their individual powers are not interchangeable.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- **Light microscope:** uses visible light.\n- **Compound microscope:** series of lenses; transmitted-light specimens must be thin enough for light to pass through.\n- **Dissecting microscope:** allows opaque specimens to be viewed.\n- **Eyepiece / ocular lens:** top viewing lens, commonly **10× or 15×**.\n- **Monocular:** one eyepiece. **Binocular:** two eyepieces.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Visible light → transmitted illumination → thin specimen:** Light must pass through a specimen for conventional transmitted-light compound microscopy. **Opaque specimen → dissecting microscope:** External surfaces can be examined without transmitted light. **Objective lens + ocular lens → compound magnification:** Both lens systems contribute to image enlargement. **Lens design → aberration control:** Lens composition and curvature influence image quality.\n\n**Optional check:** Which microscope permits opaque specimens to be viewed? **Answer:** A dissecting microscope.\n\n\n---","references":["(PDF p.53)","(p.54)","(pp.55–60)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_05","title":"Lesson 5 — Magnification, Parfocality and Working Distance","markdown":"# Lesson 5 — Magnification, Parfocality and Working Distance\n\n## Two functions of microscopy\nMicroscopes perform two related but distinct functions:\n1. **Magnification:** making the specimen appear larger.\n2. **Resolution:** making two nearby objects distinguishable as separate objects.\n\nIncreasing magnification without improving discernible detail is of little advantage. This is called **empty magnification** when enlargement increases without a corresponding improvement in resolution.\n\n## Magnification\n**Magnification** is the ratio of the size of the final image to the actual size of the specimen. It depends on lens focal lengths and their arrangement.\n\nThe slide values to remember are:\n- Ordinary glass microscopes: approximately **25×–1500×**.\n- Some compound microscopes: useful magnification up to **2000×** with an oil-immersion lens.\n- Most dissecting microscopes: magnification up to approximately **30×**.\n- Above **2000×** for compound light microscopes is generally considered **empty magnification** in this course.\n\n**Understanding note:** These are the course's stated approximate ranges, not universal specifications for every modern microscope.\n\nFor a standard compound microscope, **total magnification = eyepiece magnification × objective magnification**. For example, a 10× eyepiece and 40× objective produce 400× total magnification.\n\n## Parfocality\n**Parfocal objectives** remain approximately in focus when changing from a lower-power objective (such as 10×) to a higher-power objective (such as 40×). Only minor focusing adjustments should be needed.\n\n## Working distance\n**Working distance** is the distance between the front of the objective lens and the specimen. It is relatively long at low magnification and generally becomes shorter as magnification increases.\n\n**Oil-immersion objectives** have a very short working distance and may come extremely close to the specimen. Avoid pressing the lens against the slide or specimen, as this can damage both.\n\n**Understanding note:** On a typical covered slide, immersion oil occupies the gap between the front of the oil-immersion objective lens and the coverslip, rather than requiring the glass lens to touch the specimen.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- **Two functions:** magnification and improved resolution.\n- **Magnification:** image-size/specimen-size ratio; depends on focal lengths and lens arrangement.\n- **25×–1500×:** ordinary glass microscope range; **2000×:** some compound microscopes with oil immersion.\n- **~30×:** most dissecting microscopes.\n- **Above 2000×:** generally empty magnification for compound microscopes in the course.\n- **Parfocal:** objectives remain approximately in focus after changing magnification.\n- **Working distance decreases as magnification increases** (general rule).\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Magnification ≠ resolution:** An enlarged blur does not reveal more detail. **Higher magnification → shorter working distance:** The objective comes closer to the specimen. **Parfocal objectives → efficient objective switching:** The image stays nearly focused. **Oil immersion → very short working distance:** Correct handling is essential to protect the lens and slide.\n\n**Optional check:** A 10× ocular and a 40× objective give what total magnification? **Answer:** 400×.\n\n\n---","references":["(PDF pp.61, 68–70)","(p.69)","(pp.70–72)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_06","title":"Lesson 6 — Resolution and Numerical Aperture","markdown":"# Lesson 6 — Resolution and Numerical Aperture\n\n## Resolving power\n**Resolution (resolving power)** is the ability to distinguish two closely spaced objects as separate. As objects move closer together, a point is reached where they can no longer be seen as distinct; a microscope with better resolving power can distinguish finer detail.\n\n## Factors that determine resolution\n\n### 1. Numerical aperture (NA)\n**Numerical aperture** measures an objective lens's ability to gather image-forming light and resolve fine specimen detail. Fine structures diffract light; collecting a wider cone of these rays helps reveal more detail.\n\n**NA = η sin U**\n\n- **η (eta)** = refractive index of the medium between the specimen and objective lens.\n- **U** = half-angle of the cone of light accepted by the objective lens (the slide also uses **u**).\n- **Angular aperture** = the angle of the light cone captured by the front of the objective lens.\n\nA wider collected light cone and a higher refractive index increase NA. Careful adjustment of the illumination system helps fill the objective's angular aperture.\n\nFor **dry objectives**, air is the medium (refractive index **1.00**). The theoretical NA cannot exceed **1.0** in air; in practice, dry objectives rarely exceed approximately **0.95**.\n\n**Oil immersion** uses a medium whose refractive index is closer to that of glass, reducing loss of high-angle rays and allowing higher NA. The slides give specialized immersion oil at approximately **1.51** and refer to an optimum of approximately **1.55** for glass-covered slides.\n\n**Understanding note:** Refractive index describes how much light slows in a material. A higher-NA objective can collect a wider range of rays, improving detail and often brightness.\n\n### 2. Wavelength\nResolution also depends on **wavelength (λ)**: **shorter wavelengths generally allow finer detail to be resolved**.\n\n### 3. Limit of resolution\nThe minimum separation that can be distinguished is expressed as:\n\n**r = 0.61 λ / NA**\n\nHere **r** is the limit of resolution, **λ** is wavelength and **NA** is numerical aperture. A **smaller r** means better resolution.\n\nThe slides use **NA ≈ 1.4** as a practical high-end value for light-microscope objectives and **green light at λ = 560 nm** as an example. Thus:\n\n**r ≈ (0.61 × 560 nm) / 1.4 = 244 nm ≈ 0.24 µm.**\n\nThe slides also give the approximate rule **r ≈ λ/2** and use a typical visible wavelength of about **600 nm**, corresponding to an order-of-magnitude resolution limit near **300 nm**.\n\n**Understanding note:** The exact resolution estimate depends on the optical setup and the formula or approximation used; do not treat every quoted value as identical.\n\n## Objective correction and numerical aperture\nMore highly corrected objectives may have larger NA at the same magnification. For **10× objectives**, the slide values are:\n\n| Objective type | NA |\n|---|---:|\n| Plan achromat | **0.25** |\n| Plan fluorite | **0.30** |\n| Plan apochromat | **0.45** |\n\nThe full slide table includes further objective magnifications and NA values; it should be provided as a verified reference figure rather than silently omitted.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- **Resolution:** distinguishing two nearby objects as separate.\n- **NA = η sin U**; η = refractive index; U = half-angle of accepted light cone.\n- **Higher NA → generally better resolution** and more collected light.\n- **Air refractive index ≈ 1.00**; specialized immersion oil ≈ **1.51**; glass-covered-slide optimum cited ≈ **1.55**.\n- **Dry-objective NA:** theoretical maximum **1.0**, practical approximately **0.95**.\n- **Shorter wavelength → better resolution**.\n- **r = 0.61 λ / NA**; smaller r means finer resolvable detail.\n- **High-end objective NA ≈ 1.4**; green-light example **560 nm**.\n- **10× objective NA:** plan achromat **0.25**, plan fluorite **0.30**, plan apochromat **0.45**.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Fine detail → diffracted light → angular aperture:** Collecting a wider cone of image-forming light reveals more detail. **Refractive index + angular aperture → NA:** Both determine the light-gathering capacity of an objective. **Higher NA + shorter wavelength → smaller r:** The minimum distinguishable separation decreases. **Oil immersion → increased NA:** An appropriate immersion medium permits high-angle rays to reach the objective. **Aberration correction → usable high NA:** Corrected lens systems help maintain image quality while collecting more light.\n\n**Optional check:** If wavelength stays constant but NA doubles, what happens to r? **Answer:** It halves, so resolution improves.","references":["(PDF pp.80–86)","(pp.87–89)","(pp.91–96)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_07","title":"Lesson 7 — Bright-field and Dark-field Microscopy","markdown":"# Lesson 7 — Bright-field and Dark-field Microscopy\n\n### Bright-field microscopy\n\n**Bright-field microscopy** produces a generally dark or coloured specimen against a bright background. Light from a lamp is gathered by the **substage condenser** (the lens system below the specimen) and focused onto the specimen. The image depends on how the specimen changes the light passing through it.\n\n**Contrast** means a visible difference in brightness or colour between a specimen and its surroundings. It can arise from differences in light absorption and refractive index. A **refractive index** describes how strongly a material slows light; specimens with optical properties similar to their surroundings can be difficult to see. Staining can increase contrast, but many conventional staining procedures kill or disrupt living cells.\n\n**Limitation:** Thin, transparent, unstained living cells may be difficult to examine by ordinary bright-field microscopy.\n\n### Dark-field microscopy\n\n**Dark-field microscopy** shows a bright specimen against a dark background. A modified condenser contains an **opaque disc** that blocks direct light from entering the objective lens (the lens closest to the specimen). Light scattered by the specimen enters the objective lens and forms the image.\n\nDark-field microscopy is useful for **living, unstained microorganisms**, especially specimens difficult to see or unsuitable for conventional staining. Examples include microorganisms in liquid suspensions, such as yeast cells, and material from tissue cultures.\n\n**Remember:** A bright object against a black background is characteristic of dark-field illumination. Dark-field improves visibility; it does not automatically improve the microscope's fundamental resolving power.\n\n### Bright-field versus dark-field\n\n| Feature | Bright-field | Dark-field |\n|---|---|---|\n| Background | Bright | Dark |\n| Appearance of specimen | Darker or stained | Bright or luminous |\n| Illumination | Direct transmitted light contributes to image | Direct light blocked; scattered light contributes |\n| Main challenge/advantage | Transparent cells may lack contrast | Unstained, low-contrast specimens become easier to see |\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- Bright-field: substage condenser gathers lamp light and focuses it on the specimen.\n- Visibility depends on specimen contrast, refractive index and opacity.\n- Staining can improve contrast but may kill or disturb living cells.\n- Dark-field: opaque disc in a dark-field condenser blocks direct light from the objective lens.\n- Specimen appears bright against a dark background; useful for live, unstained microorganisms.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Condenser → illumination:** controls light reaching the specimen. **Refractive index → contrast:** optical differences affect visibility. **Dark-field → scattering:** light redirected by a specimen becomes visible against a dark background. **Phase contrast (next lesson) → transparent cells:** converts otherwise hard-to-see optical differences into visible contrast.\n\n**Optional check:** Why can a transparent living cell be difficult to see under bright-field illumination? *Because it may differ very little in contrast from the surrounding medium.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_08","title":"Lesson 8 — Phase-Contrast Microscopy","markdown":"# Lesson 8 — Phase-Contrast Microscopy\n\n### Why phase contrast is needed\n\nLiving cells and their internal structures are often almost transparent. They may absorb very little light and therefore show poor contrast in ordinary bright-field microscopy. Staining can improve visibility, but may interfere with the living cell.\n\n**Phase-contrast microscopy** makes transparent, unstained living cells easier to see by converting differences in the *phase* of light into differences in brightness.\n\n### How it works\n\nLight passing through a specimen emerges as **direct (undiffracted) rays** and **diffracted or scattered rays**. Passage through different parts of the specimen changes the timing, or **phase**, of the light waves. These differences are usually too small to produce strong visible contrast.\n\nA phase-contrast microscope uses two important optical components:\n\n1. An **annulus**, or ring-shaped aperture, beneath the stage.\n2. A **phase plate** within the objective lens.\n\nThese components increase the brightness differences formed when direct and diffracted light interfere (combine). In the positive phase-contrast arrangement described here, parts of the specimen appear darker than the brighter background.\n\n**Useful distinction:** *Wavelength* is related to the colour of light; *amplitude* is related to its intensity or brightness. *Phase* describes the position of a wave within its cycle.\n\n### Applications and limitations\n\nPhase-contrast microscopy is useful for observing living cells and tissues, including **cell movement, nuclei, organelles and cytoplasmic flow**. It can be combined with **time-lapse photography** to follow changes over time.\n\nIts resolution remains limited by the wavelength of visible light. Improving contrast is not the same as removing the optical resolution limit.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- Living cells are often colourless, transparent and difficult to see with ordinary bright-field illumination.\n- Phase contrast makes small phase changes visible as brightness differences.\n- Light is divided conceptually into direct and diffracted rays.\n- Special **annulus below the stage** and **phase plates in the objectives** enhance phase differences.\n- Applications: live-cell observation, cell movement, nuclear/organellar details, cytoplasmic flow and time-lapse imaging.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Transparent cell → weak absorption contrast.** **Specimen thickness/refractive index → phase change.** **Annulus + phase plate → interference and brightness contrast.** **Phase contrast versus staining → study living cells without routine destructive stains.**\n\n**Optional check:** Where is the phase plate located? *In the objective lens.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_09","title":"Lesson 9 — Ultraviolet Microscopy","markdown":"# Lesson 9 — Ultraviolet Microscopy\n\n### Principle\n\n**Ultraviolet (UV) microscopy** uses ultraviolet light, which has a shorter wavelength than visible light. Shorter wavelengths can improve resolution; an improvement of **approximately twofold** is sometimes described compared with white-light microscopy.\n\nSome specimens absorb UV light naturally. Other specimens can be treated with dyes that absorb UV and emit light at a longer wavelength. This light emission is closely connected to **fluorescence microscopy**, covered next.\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n1. **Ordinary glass lenses absorb much UV light**, so suitable UV-transmitting optical materials, such as quartz, may be needed; these are expensive.\n2. **UV light is invisible to the human eye**, so the UV image cannot simply be viewed directly in the usual way.\n3. **UV exposure can damage the eyes**, requiring protective optical arrangements, cameras or a suitable viewing screen.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- UV has a shorter wavelength than visible light and may offer roughly twice the resolution in the comparison presented.\n- Quartz optics may be required instead of ordinary glass.\n- UV cannot be seen directly and can harm the eyes; indirect imaging is necessary.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Wavelength → resolution:** shorter wavelength can reveal finer detail. **UV excitation → fluorescence:** some molecules emit longer-wavelength light after absorbing UV. **Optical material → transmission:** the lens must transmit the wavelengths being used.\n\n**Optional check:** Why are quartz lenses used in some UV microscopes? *Quartz can transmit UV wavelengths that ordinary glass absorbs.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_10","title":"Lesson 10 — Fluorescence and Immunofluorescence","markdown":"# Lesson 10 — Fluorescence and Immunofluorescence\n\n### Fluorescence microscopy\n\n**Fluorescence** occurs when a substance absorbs light of one wavelength and emits light at a longer wavelength. A **fluorescence microscope** uses illumination and filters to excite fluorescent substances and detect the light they emit.\n\nSome cellular substances show **natural fluorescence**. Other structures can be labelled with fluorescent dyes called **fluorochromes**. The labelled structures then appear bright against a relatively dark background.\n\n### Excitation and emission\n\n1. **Excitation light** illuminates a fluorescent molecule and raises it to a higher-energy state.\n2. The molecule returns towards a lower-energy state and emits light at a **longer wavelength**.\n3. Optical filters help ensure that mainly the emitted light reaches the observer or camera.\n\nA **dichroic mirror** separates excitation light from emitted fluorescence by reflecting selected wavelengths and transmitting others.\n\n### Epifluorescence\n\nIn **epifluorescence microscopy**, the **objective lens** (the lens nearest the specimen) both directs excitation light towards the specimen and collects the emitted fluorescent light. The microscope must separate the incoming and outgoing light efficiently to avoid a bright background obscuring the signal.\n\n### Immunofluorescence\n\n**Immunofluorescence** uses an **antibody** that binds a particular cellular protein and carries a fluorescent label, such as **fluorescein**. When the labelled antibody binds its target, the corresponding structure fluoresces under suitable illumination.\n\nThis allows specific molecules or cell structures to be located rather than merely showing the outline of a cell.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- Fluorescence: absorption of shorter-wavelength light followed by emission at a longer wavelength.\n- **Fluorochromes** are fluorescent dyes used to label otherwise non-fluorescent structures.\n- **Epifluorescence:** objective lens both focuses excitation light and collects emitted fluorescence.\n- **Dichroic mirror:** separates excitation and emission light paths.\n- **Immunofluorescence:** fluorescently labelled antibodies identify specific proteins or structures; **fluorescein** is an example of a label.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**UV microscopy → shorter-wavelength illumination.** **Fluorescence → excitation followed by longer-wavelength emission.** **Dichroic mirror + filters → clearer fluorescent signal.** **Antigen–antibody binding → selective identification of cellular proteins.**\n\n**Optional check:** What is the role of a dichroic mirror? *It helps separate excitation light from fluorescence emission.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_11","title":"Lesson 11 — Electron Microscopy and Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM)","markdown":"# Lesson 11 — Electron Microscopy and Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM)\n\n### Why electrons?\n\nElectrons have both particle-like and wave-like properties. When accelerated through a voltage, they have a very short wavelength. This gives electron microscopy the potential to resolve details far smaller than those normally visible with a light microscope.\n\n**Slide formula:** Electron wavelength is presented as **λ ≈ √(1.5/V) nm**, where **V** is the accelerating voltage in volts. For **60 kV**, the slide gives a wavelength of approximately **0.005 nm**.\n\n**Important clarification:** This is an approximate relationship; the extremely short electron wavelength does not by itself guarantee equally small practical resolution. Lens imperfections, specimen contrast and preparation quality also matter.\n\nTwo major types of electron microscope are **transmission electron microscopes (TEM)** and **scanning electron microscopes (SEM)**.\n\n### How TEM works\n\nA **transmission electron microscope** passes a focused electron beam through an **ultrathin section** of a specially prepared specimen.\n\n- An **electron gun** produces electrons; a heated cathode and an anode are part of the system described here.\n- The electrons are accelerated by a high voltage; the slide gives **50–150 kV** as an example range.\n- **Electromagnetic lenses** (coils producing magnetic fields) focus the beam.\n- A **condenser lens** focuses electrons towards the specimen.\n- Electrons interact with the specimen; different regions scatter electrons differently.\n- An **objective lens** and further **projector lenses** form and enlarge the image.\n- The image may be viewed on a fluorescent screen or recorded.\n\n### Specimen contrast and practical resolution\n\nBiological specimens contain many low-atomic-number elements such as **carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen**, so they naturally produce relatively weak electron contrast. Preparation techniques strongly influence the final image.\n\nThe slides quote **0.2–0.3 nm** resolution for certain electron microscopes and **approximately 300,000×** as a useful magnification figure. Treat these as lecture-specific examples, not universal specifications for every TEM.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- Electrons have wave properties; electron wavelength decreases with increased accelerating voltage.\n- Slide formula: **λ ≈ √(1.5/V) nm**; **60 kV → about 0.005 nm**.\n- Electron microscopes: **TEM** and **SEM**.\n- **TEM** sends electrons through an **ultrathin specimen**.\n- Electron gun, cathode/anode, condenser, electromagnetic objective and projector lenses are key components.\n- Biological material gives weak contrast because it largely contains low-atomic-number elements.\n- Resolution depends on lens quality, contrast and specimen preparation, not wavelength alone.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**Accelerating voltage → electron wavelength.** **Electron wavelength → potential resolution.** **Specimen thickness → electron transmission.** **Atomic composition → electron scattering and image contrast.** **Preparation quality → practical image detail.**\n\n**Optional check:** Why must a TEM specimen be ultrathin? *Electrons need to pass through it to form a transmission image.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_12","title":"Lesson 12 — Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and Microscope Comparison","markdown":"# Lesson 12 — Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and Microscope Comparison\n\n### Scanning electron microscopy\n\nA **scanning electron microscope (SEM)** uses a beam of electrons to examine the **surface** of a specimen. The electron beam scans across the surface, and detected signals are used to construct an image.\n\nSEM is particularly useful for the surface details of **solid specimens**. The images often have a striking **three-dimensional appearance** because a relatively large depth of the specimen remains in focus.\n\n**Depth of field** means the range of distances that appear acceptably sharp in one image.\n\nApplications include biological surfaces, foreign particles, residues, polymers, electronic components and fractured metal surfaces.\n\n### Features and figures to remember\n\n- Large depth of field compared with ordinary light microscopy.\n- High magnification and resolution for detailed surface examination.\n- Slides give examples of **up to 100,000× magnification**, and a typical SEM figure of **about 10 nm resolution and 20,000× magnification** unless field-emission SEM (**FESEM**) is used.\n- SEM images are **3D-looking**, but a standard SEM image is not automatically a true three-dimensional measurement.\n\n### TEM versus SEM\n\n| Feature | TEM | SEM |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it mainly examines | Internal ultrastructure of thin specimens | Specimen surface |\n| Electron interaction | Electrons pass through specimen | Beam scans surface; emitted/scattered signals detected |\n| Image appearance | Fine internal detail, generally 2D projection | Surface detail, often 3D-looking |\n| Specimen preparation | Ultrathin sections | Suitable solid specimen preparation |\n\n### Light versus electron microscopes\n\n| Feature | Light microscope | Electron microscope |\n|---|---|---|\n| Illumination | Visible light | Electron beam |\n| Lenses | Glass optics | Electromagnetic lenses |\n| Image | Colour possible | Initially monochrome signal/image |\n| Resolution | Limited by visible-light wavelength | Can be substantially higher |\n| Example maximum magnification in slides | **1,000–1,500×** | **300,000–500,000×** (comparison table) |\n| Example resolving power in slides | **~240 nm** | **~0.2 nm** |\n\nThe slide comparison gives representative figures, not guaranteed limits of every instrument. **Magnification** enlarges an image; **resolution** determines whether nearby details can be distinguished.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n- SEM examines the **surface of solid specimens**.\n- SEM offers a **large depth of field** and produces **3D-looking surface images**.\n- Slide figures: **100,000×** as an SEM magnification example; **10 nm and 20,000×** for a typical SEM; FESEM may perform better.\n- TEM: internal detail through thin sections. SEM: surface topography.\n- Light versus electron comparison: **glass versus electromagnetic lenses**, visible light versus electrons, and the slide-specific magnification/resolution figures above.\n\n### Concept Connections\n**SEM → surface topography.** **TEM → internal ultrastructure.** **Depth of field → 3D-looking surface images.** **Resolution versus magnification → image detail versus image size.** **Microscope selection → biological question being investigated.**\n\n**Optional check:** Which electron microscope would you choose to examine the surface of a pollen grain? *SEM.*\n\n\n\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_13","title":"Lesson 13 — Why Specimens Must Be Prepared","markdown":"# Lesson 13 — Why Specimens Must Be Prepared\n\n### The preparation sequence\n\nCells and tissues cannot always be examined directly. Some are too thick for light or electron transmission, and transparent structures may lack sufficient contrast. Preparing a specimen helps preserve its structure, provide mechanical support, produce suitably thin sections and make important components visible.\n\nThe main procedures are **fixation → embedding → sectioning → staining**. **Dehydration** and **clearing** are essential steps within many embedding workflows.\n\n- **Fixation:** rapidly kills and preserves tissue, reducing breakdown and changes in structure.\n- **Dehydration:** removes water, commonly using progressively stronger alcohol solutions.\n- **Embedding:** surrounds or infiltrates tissue with a supporting material, such as paraffin wax or epoxy resin.\n- **Sectioning:** cuts thin slices so light or electrons can pass through appropriately.\n- **Staining:** increases contrast between structures that would otherwise look similar.\n\n### Why thickness matters\n\nFor **transmission electron microscopy (TEM)**, electrons must pass through the section. Excessively thick tissue scatters electrons and obscures detail. Thin sections are therefore essential. The sectioning range given for electron microscopy is **50–100 nm**, with **approximately 50 nm** also highlighted.\n\n**Concept Connections:** Preservation (fixation) prevents tissue deterioration; support (embedding) makes thin cutting possible; thinness (sectioning) allows transmission; contrast (staining) makes components distinguishable. These stages solve different problems and work together.\n\n**Slide Essentials:** Four named procedures: fixation, embedding, sectioning, staining. Dehydration precedes embedding. TEM needs ultrathin sections because the electron beam must pass through the specimen.\n\n**Optional check:** Why can't an ordinary thick tissue block be placed directly in a TEM? *Too many electrons are scattered, reducing transmission and image detail.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_14","title":"Lesson 14 — Fixation","markdown":"# Lesson 14 — Fixation\n\n### Meaning and purpose\n\n**Fixation** is the rapid killing and preservation of tissue. It is fundamental to histological preparation because it helps prevent **autolysis** (self-digestion by cellular enzymes), bacterial or fungal attack and damage during subsequent procedures.\n\nFixation may be **physical** or **chemical**.\n\n### Physical fixation\n\nA specimen can be frozen rapidly by immersion in **liquid nitrogen**, a cryogenic liquid at approximately **−196 °C (−320 °F)**. Rapid freezing aims to minimise formation of damaging ice crystals and preserve tissue structure and enzymatic components.\n\n**Important:** Frozen tissue remains preserved only while kept appropriately frozen. If it warms, autolysis can resume. Permanent preparations of frozen sections may therefore require chemical fixation.\n\n### Chemical fixation\n\nChemical fixatives stabilise structural components, particularly **proteins and lipids**. Fixatives for electron microscopy should preserve organelles without producing deposits that obscure their structure.\n\n| Light microscopy: listed fixatives | Electron microscopy: listed fixatives |\n|---|---|\n| Acetic acid, alcohol, acetone, mercuric chloride, chromic acid, formaldehyde | Formaldehyde and glutaraldehyde (protein fixation); osmium tetroxide and potassium permanganate (lipid fixation) |\n\n**Understanding note:** The categories above preserve the slide's examples. In practice, the effects of a fixative depend on its concentration, mixture and protocol; osmium tetroxide is particularly useful for membrane preservation and electron contrast.\n\n### Concept Connections\n\n**Autolysis → fixation:** preservation prevents tissue from digesting itself. **Liquid nitrogen → rapid freezing:** fast cooling reduces ice-crystal damage. **Protein/lipid stabilisation → organelle preservation:** chemical fixation protects the structures that later microscopy aims to reveal.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n\n- Fixation: *rapid killing and preservation of a tissue*.\n- Prevents autolysis, microbial attack and damage during processing.\n- Physical and chemical methods.\n- Liquid nitrogen: **−196 °C**.\n- Protein-associated fixatives: **formaldehyde, glutaraldehyde**; lipid-associated: **osmium tetroxide, potassium permanganate**.\n\n**Optional check:** Why might a frozen section deteriorate when allowed to warm? *Freezing preserves it while frozen; autolysis can resume on thawing.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_15","title":"Lesson 15 — Dehydration, Clearing and Embedding","markdown":"# Lesson 15 — Dehydration, Clearing and Embedding\n\n### Why embedding is needed\n\nSoft biological material often lacks the strength needed for thin sectioning. **Embedding** gives the specimen support so it can be cut without collapsing or tearing. The supporting medium depends on the required section thickness.\n\n### Paraffin wax preparation\n\nA typical wax-embedding sequence is:\n\n1. **Fix** the specimen.\n2. **Dehydrate** it through increasing concentrations of ethanol, ending with absolute alcohol.\n3. **Clear** it with **xylene**, which replaces the alcohol and is compatible with molten wax.\n4. **Infiltrate** the specimen with molten **paraffin wax**, replacing the clearing agent.\n5. Allow the wax to solidify to provide support for sectioning.\n\n**Clearing** is the replacement of the dehydrating alcohol with a solvent such as xylene; it is not simply washing the specimen.\n\n### Epoxy resin preparation\n\nFor very thin sections, a harder medium such as **epoxy resin (e.g., Araldite)** provides greater support. A resin solvent such as **propylene oxide** may replace xylene; after infiltration, the resin is heated to **polymerise** (harden through a chemical reaction).\n\nFor relatively thick plant sections, material such as **raw carrot or unripe pawpaw** may provide temporary mechanical support.\n\n### Example processing schedule\n\nThe following is a *specific example from the teaching material*, not a universal laboratory protocol:\n\n| Stage | Reagent | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dehydration | 70% alcohol | 60 min |\n| Dehydration | 90% alcohol | 45 min |\n| Dehydration | Absolute alcohol | 45 min |\n| Dehydration | Absolute alcohol | 45 min |\n| Dehydration | Absolute alcohol | 60 min |\n| Clearing | Xylene | 60 min × 3 |\n| Infiltration | Paraffin wax | 30 min, then 60 min, then 90 min |\n\n**Concept Connections:** Water removal → alcohol dehydration; alcohol removal → xylene clearing; xylene replacement → wax infiltration; harder resin → support for ultrathin electron-microscopy sections.\n\n**Slide Essentials:** Graded ethanol up to **100%**, **xylene** for wax processing, **propylene oxide** for the resin workflow, **paraffin wax** versus **epoxy resin/Araldite**, and the specific sequence and times in this lesson.\n\n**Optional check:** What is the role of xylene in wax embedding? *It replaces alcohol and permits subsequent infiltration by molten paraffin wax.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_16","title":"Lesson 16 — Sectioning and Microtomes","markdown":"# Lesson 16 — Sectioning and Microtomes\n\n### Sectioning\n\n**Sectioning** is cutting a prepared specimen into thin slices for microscopy. A **microtome** is the instrument used to cut sections of controlled thickness.\n\nThe section thicknesses listed are:\n\n- **Light microscopy:** **1,000–20,000 nm** (1–20 µm).\n- **Electron microscopy:** **50–100 nm**, with approximately **50 nm** emphasised for ultrathin TEM sections.\n\n### Essential parts of a microtome\n\nAll microtomes include:\n\n1. A **specimen holder**.\n2. A **sharp cutting edge**.\n3. A **mechanism for regulating section thickness**.\n\n**Knife selection:** Steel razor blades are described for wax-embedded specimens; **glass or diamond knives** are used for hard resin-embedded specimens such as Araldite preparations.\n\n**Frozen sections:** A microtome operating inside a freezer at approximately **−20 °C** is used for frozen material. This is commonly called a **cryostat microtome**.\n\n### Mounting sections\n\n- **Light microscopy:** sections are mounted on **glass microscope slides**.\n- **Electron microscopy:** ultrathin sections are supported on **fine copper grids**.\n\n### Concept Connections\n\n**Embedding → sectioning:** the support medium prevents delicate tissue from deforming. **Microtome → reproducible thickness:** controlled slicing makes specimens suitable for examination. **Glass slide versus copper grid → different imaging systems:** light microscopy and TEM require different section supports.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n\n**Microtome = specimen holder + sharp cutting edge + thickness regulator.** Steel razor for wax, glass/diamond knife for resin; frozen-section microtome at **−20 °C**; light-microscopy slides versus electron-microscopy copper grids; section thicknesses **1–20 µm** and **50–100 nm**.\n\n**Optional check:** Which cutting edge is suitable for hard resin-embedded material? *A glass or diamond knife.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_17","title":"Lesson 17 — Staining for Light Microscopy","markdown":"# Lesson 17 — Staining for Light Microscopy\n\n### Why stain tissues?\n\nMany tissue sections are almost transparent. **Staining** increases contrast so that cells and their components can be distinguished.\n\nCommon light-microscopy stains include organic dyes. Some, such as **toluidine blue**, stain broadly; others preferentially bind to particular cell components.\n\n### Basic and acidic stains\n\nStain behaviour depends partly on **electrical charge**.\n\n| Stain type | Charge of dye component | Main binding tendency | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Basic stain** | Positively charged (**cationic**) | Negatively charged structures, including nucleic acids | Haematoxylin (in this course’s classification), crystal violet, methylene blue, safranin, basic fuchsin |\n| **Acidic stain** | Negatively charged (**anionic**) | Positively charged groups, especially many cytoplasmic proteins | Eosin, nigrosine, picric acid, acid fuchsin, India ink (listed among examples) |\n\n**Understanding note:** Haematoxylin is commonly used with a mordant to form a positively charged staining complex; the practical result is strong nuclear staining. Some listed materials, including India ink, are better understood as specialised contrast agents rather than simple acidic dyes.\n\n### Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E)\n\n**Haematoxylin** preferentially colours nuclei blue-purple because of their nucleic-acid-rich material. **Eosin** colours many cytoplasmic and extracellular proteins pink to red. Together, they form a routine stain combination for many animal tissues.\n\n### Concept Connections\n\n**Charge → selective staining:** opposite charges attract. **Nucleic acids → nuclear basophilia:** nuclei bind the haematoxylin staining complex. **Protein-rich cytoplasm → eosinophilia:** cytoplasmic components often bind eosin. **Staining → contrast:** dyes make transparent sections interpretable.\n\n### Slide Essentials\n\n- Broad stain: **toluidine blue**.\n- **Basic stains** bind mainly negatively charged structures; **acidic stains** bind mainly positively charged structures.\n- Basic examples: **haematoxylin, crystal violet, methylene blue, safranin, basic fuchsin**.\n- Acidic examples listed: **nigrosine, picric acid, eosin, acid fuchsin, India ink**.\n- **H&E** is routinely used for animal tissues; nuclei blue-purple and much cytoplasm pink-red.\n\n**Optional check:** Why does eosin commonly stain cytoplasm? *Its negatively charged dye binds positively charged groups in cytoplasmic proteins.*\n\n---\n\n---","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"lesson_18","title":"Lesson 18 — Staining for Electron Microscopy","markdown":"# Lesson 18 — Staining for Electron Microscopy\n\n### Electron contrast rather than colour\n\nElectron micrographs do not use colour dyes to create the primary image. Electron staining increases **electron contrast**: areas that scatter or absorb more electrons appear more **electron-dense** (typically darker in conventional TEM images).\n\nHeavy elements with high atomic numbers are useful because they interact strongly with electrons. Common examples include **lead (Pb)** and **uranium (U)**.\n\n### Heavy-metal staining\n\n- **Uranium compounds:** described as binding preferentially to **nucleic acids and proteins**.\n- **Lead compounds:** described as binding to **lipids and membranes**.\n- **Osmium:** contributes electron density to **membranes**, as well as other cellular components.\n\nThese associations are useful study summaries, not exclusive binding rules: actual staining patterns depend on reagent and specimen preparation.\n\n### Positive and negative staining\n\n**Positive staining** increases the electron density of specimen structures so they appear dark relative to their surroundings. Heavy-metal staining can highlight membranes, organelles and cellular material.\n\n**Negative staining** makes the *background* electron-dense, leaving the specimen relatively light. It is especially useful for small particles such as **viruses, ribosomes and isolated organelle or membrane fragments**.\n\nNegative-stain reagents listed include **potassium phosphotungstate, uranyl acetate and ammonium molybdate**.\n\n### Important comparison\n\n| Light microscopy | Electron microscopy |\n|---|---|\n| Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E) | Uranium and lead double staining |\n| Colour differences make structures visible | Electron-density differences make structures visible |\n| Glass slides | Fine copper grids for ultrathin sections |\n\n### Concept Connections\n\n**High atomic number → stronger electron scattering → greater contrast.** **Positive staining → darker specimen structures.** **Negative staining → dark background and light specimen.** **TEM sectioning + staining → transmitted-electron image with useful detail.**\n\n### Slide Essentials\n\n- Heavy-metal contrast agents: **uranium, lead and osmium**.\n- Uranium: nucleic acids/proteins; lead: lipids/membranes (slide associations).\n- **Positive stain:** specimen darker. **Negative stain:** background darker.\n- Negative stains: **potassium phosphotungstate, uranyl acetate, ammonium molybdate**.\n- Uranium + lead is compared with **H&E** in light microscopy.\n\n**Optional check:** In negative staining, which area appears electron-dense? *The background around the specimen.*","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CO01","title":"The Cell as the Fundamental Unit of Life","markdown":"# The Cell as the Fundamental Unit of Life\n\n**Learning mission:** Define a cell, identify its fundamental properties, and explain why cells are the basis of organismal life.\n\n## 1. What is a cell?\nA **cell** is the smallest living unit capable of carrying out the functions of life. The lecture defines it as *the smallest unit of life capable of independently performing life functions* . A cell is the **basic living, structural and functional unit of life** .\n\nThe distinction matters: a tissue contains cells, but the individual cell is the fundamental living unit. Multicellular organisms contain many cells arranged into complex structures; unicellular organisms carry out life functions within a single cell .\n\n## 2. Properties of living cells\nCells can **grow, reproduce, process information, respond to stimuli and carry out chemical reactions** . These capabilities underpin life. Not every specialized cell reproduces independently: mature human red blood cells, for example, cannot divide. Thus, the lecture's wording describes the general capacity of cellular life, not a universal property of every mature cell.\n\n## 3. Why organismal function depends on cells\nThe activity of an organism depends on the **individual and collective activities of its cells**. A cell's biochemical activities depend on its **subcellular structures** . For example, mitochondria support aerobic ATP production, while the plasma membrane regulates exchange. These examples explain the principle; the organelles are developed in later lessons.\n\n## 4. General functions of cells\nThe lecture identifies four:\n\n1. **Compartmentalization:** organize chemical reactions within specialized structures.\n2. **Regulation of inflow and outflow:** selectively exchange substances with the surroundings.\n3. **Use of genetic material:** direct cellular activities through stored biological information.\n4. **Continuity of life:** new cells arise from pre-existing cells, transmitting biological information.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Cell = basic living structural and functional unit of life.\n- Unicellular organisms perform life functions in one cell; multicellular organisms coordinate many cells.\n- Cells grow, reproduce, process information, respond to stimuli and carry out chemical reactions (general properties).\n- The four general functions are compartmentalization, exchange regulation, genetic direction and continuity.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Cell → tissue → organ:** higher levels of organization rely on cells. **Plasma membrane → regulated exchange.** **Genetic material → cellular control.** **Subcellular structure → biochemical function.**","references":["(PDF p. 193)","(p. 194)","(p. 192)","(p. 191)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CO02","title":"Cellular Diversity, Size and Shape","markdown":"# Cellular Diversity, Size and Shape\n\n**Learning mission:** Relate cellular structure to function and identify factors controlling cell shape.\n\n## 1. Cellular diversity\nCells differ in **size, shape and function**. The lecture states that the human body contains approximately **100 trillion cells** and **200 cell types** . These are the slide's approximate teaching figures, not precise modern counts: more recent estimates put total cells in a typical adult in the tens of trillions, with totals varying by body size and counting method. The number of distinguishable cell types also depends on classification.\n\nThe key examinable relationship is **form follows function**: cellular shape and internal organization suit the work the cell performs.\n\n## 2. The typical cell\nThe slide labelled **A typical cell**  introduces the general plan of a eukaryotic cell. The broad components are the **plasma membrane**, **cytoplasm** and **nucleus**. These are introduced here; detailed membrane, nucleus and organelle structure belongs in subsequent modules. Refer to the accompanying original slide figure for the specific labels used by the lecturer.\n\n## 3. What determines cell shape?\nThe lecture  lists five influences:\n\n1. **Surface tension of the cytoplasm** — a lecture term for forces influencing cellular form; in modern cell biology, cortical tension at the cell surface is especially relevant.\n2. **Viscosity of the cytoplasm** — resistance to internal flow and deformation.\n3. **Mechanical action of adjoining cells** — pushing, pulling and contacts with neighbours.\n4. **Rigidity of the membrane** — the lecture's wording; membrane-associated cytoskeleton and, where present, the cell wall are major sources of mechanical support.\n5. **Functional adaptation** — specialization for the cell's role.\n\nA cell's shape is not determined by a single factor. Its internal scaffolding, environment and function interact.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Cell size and shape vary with function.\n- Lecture estimate: ~100 trillion cells and ~200 types (historical approximations).\n- Shape factors: cytoplasmic surface tension, viscosity, adjoining-cell forces, membrane rigidity and functional adaptation.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Shape ↔ function:** specialization changes cellular geometry. **Cytoskeleton ↔ mechanical stability.** **Membrane ↔ boundary and exchange.** **Cell size ↔ surface-area-to-volume ratio** (next lesson).","references":["(PDF p. 197)","(p. 198)","(p. 200)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CO03","title":"Surface-Area-to-Volume Ratio and Cell Size","markdown":"# Surface-Area-to-Volume Ratio and Cell Size\n\n**Learning mission:** Calculate surface area and volume of cubic models and explain why cell size is constrained.\n\n## 1. The geometric principle\nFor a cube with side length **L**:\n\n- **Surface area = 6L²**\n- **Volume = L³**\n- **Surface-area-to-volume ratio = 6/L**, with units of inverse length (e.g. cm⁻¹).\n\nThe ratio falls as the cube becomes larger. Surface area grows with the **square** of length, while volume grows with the **cube** of length.\n\n## 2. The lecture's worked examples \n\n| Side length | Area of one face | Volume | Total surface area | SA:V |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 cm | 1 cm² | 1 cm³ | 6 cm² | 6 cm⁻¹ |\n| 2 cm | 4 cm² | 8 cm³ | 24 cm² | 3 cm⁻¹ |\n| 4 cm | 16 cm² | 64 cm³ | 96 cm² | 1.5 cm⁻¹ |\n\n**Example:** Doubling the cube's side from 1 cm to 2 cm increases surface area fourfold (6 → 24 cm²) but volume eightfold (1 → 8 cm³). The SA:V ratio halves (6 → 3 cm⁻¹).\n\n## 3. Why most cells are microscopic\nMaterials often enter or leave across the cell's surface, while metabolic needs depend substantially on internal volume. As size increases, **the available exchange surface per unit volume decreases**. Smaller cells therefore often exchange nutrients, gases and wastes more efficiently relative to their volume. This is an important constraint, not the only factor governing cell size: transport systems, metabolic rate and cell geometry also matter.\n\n## 4. Ways cells adapt\nCells may remain small, become flattened or elongated, develop folds or projections, or use internal transport. These are explanatory applications of the geometric principle, not separate claims made on the lecture slides.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- For cubes, SA = 6L², V = L³ and SA:V = 6/L.\n- Lecture ratios for sides 1, 2 and 4 cm: **6, 3 and 1.5 cm⁻¹**.\n- Increasing linear size lowers SA:V, helping explain why most cells are microscopic.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Cell geometry → exchange capacity.** **Plasma membrane → exchange surface.** **Metabolism → volume-related demand.** **Cell specialization → modified geometry.**","references":["(PDF p. 202)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CO04","title":"Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells","markdown":"# Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells\n\n**Learning mission:** Distinguish the two major cellular organizations and explain the lecture's listed differences.\n\n## 1. Two fundamental organizations\nThe lecture introduces **prokaryotes as cells without a nucleus** and **eukaryotes as cells with a nucleus** . In eukaryotes, a membrane-bound nucleus encloses most genetic material; prokaryotes lack a membrane-bound nucleus, with DNA generally located in a nucleoid region.\n\n## 2. How the lecture distinguishes prokaryotes\nOn p. 204, the lecture groups differences into three headings:\n\n**DNA packaging**\n- No nucleus.\n- DNA described as **not wrapped around histones**. This is broadly appropriate for typical bacteria but **not universal across prokaryotes**: many archaea possess histone proteins or related DNA-packaging proteins. Preserve the lecture's point while remembering this exception.\n\n**Cell wall composition**\n- **Bacteria:** where present, cell walls commonly contain **peptidoglycan**.\n- **Archaea:** cell walls, where present, use other materials and **lack bacterial peptidoglycan**. Not all bacteria or archaea have a cell wall.\n\n**Internal organization**\n- Prokaryotes lack the **complex membrane-bound organelles** characteristic of eukaryotic cells, such as mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum. They still have organized internal structures, including ribosomes.\n\n## 3. Comparison table\n| Feature | Prokaryotic cells | Eukaryotic cells |\n|---|---|---|\n| Membrane-bound nucleus | Absent | Present in typical nucleated cells |\n| DNA location | Nucleoid region | Mostly nucleus |\n| Complex membrane-bound organelles | Absent | Present in many cell types |\n| Ribosomes | Present | Present |\n| Cell wall | Common in bacteria/archaea; composition varies | Present in plants/fungi, absent in animal cells |\n\n**Clarification:** Mature mammalian red blood cells are eukaryotic-derived cells that lack nuclei. Classification describes cellular lineage and organization, not a claim that every mature eukaryotic-derived cell retains a nucleus.\n\n## 4. Cell theory — assignment support\nThe lecture lists **Cell Theory** as an assignment  but does not present its propositions on these pages. For assignment preparation, standard cell theory states: (1) living organisms are made of one or more cells; (2) the cell is the basic unit of life; (3) cells arise from pre-existing cells. This section is **supporting textbook knowledge**, not a quotation from the slides.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Prokaryotes: no membrane-bound nucleus; eukaryotes: membrane-bound nucleus in typical cells.\n- Three slide comparison headings: **DNA packaging, cell wall makeup and internal structures**.\n- Bacterial walls: peptidoglycan where present; archaeal walls: chemically different.\n- Prokaryotes lack complex membrane-bound organelles; both groups have ribosomes.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Nucleus → DNA compartmentalization.** **Histones → DNA packaging.** **Peptidoglycan → bacterial cell wall.** **Ribosomes → protein synthesis in both groups.** **Cell theory → continuity of life.**\n\n\n## Understanding Note — cell theory assignment\nPDF p. 201 **assigns** cell theory for independent study rather than presenting its propositions. As supplementary teaching, classical cell theory states: (1) living organisms consist of one or more cells; (2) the cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life; and (3) cells arise from pre-existing cells. These statements are **not a quotation from the lecture slide**.","references":["(PDF p. 190)","(p. 201)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"OR01","title":"OR01 — Microbodies and Peroxisomes","markdown":"# OR01 — Microbodies and Peroxisomes\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## Microbodies: the family\n**Microbodies** are small, usually oval or spherical, **single-membrane-bound** organelles with specialised metabolic reactions, especially oxidation. The lecture lists **peroxisomes, glyoxysomes, hydrogenosomes and glycosomes** in this group. Microbodies are described in vertebrate liver and kidney, plant leaves and seeds, protozoa, yeasts and other fungi. These organelles are not interchangeable: their enzyme systems and functions differ.\n\n## Peroxisomes\nPeroxisomes contain oxidative enzymes. Three named in the slides are **urate oxidase, D-amino acid oxidase and catalase**.\n\n- **Urate oxidase (uricase)** participates in purine breakdown and, in organisms possessing the enzyme, converts urate toward allantoin while generating hydrogen peroxide. **Human nuance:** humans do not have a functional urate oxidase enzyme; the slide describes peroxisomal biochemistry across organisms, not an enzyme active in human liver.\n- **D-amino acid oxidase** acts on certain D-amino acids, including those that may originate from gut bacteria, producing corresponding oxoacids and hydrogen peroxide.\n- **Catalase** removes potentially harmful hydrogen peroxide: **2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂**.\n\nPeroxisomes also contribute to **fatty-acid metabolism**, amino-acid-related reactions, and detoxification. The slides mention alcohol and formaldehyde as examples of toxic substances undergoing oxidative metabolism. **Understanding note:** this does not mean peroxisomes alone perform all alcohol detoxification; several cellular pathways contribute.\n\n## How peroxisomes increase in number\nThe lecture notes that peroxisomes can form by division of pre-existing peroxisomes. They can also grow through import of proteins and lipids; modern cell biology recognises contributions from other membrane sources.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nSingle membrane; oxidative reactions; urate oxidase, D-amino acid oxidase, catalase; H₂O₂ toxicity and removal; fatty-acid metabolism; peroxisome division.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Oxidation** transfers electrons; **H₂O₂** is hydrogen peroxide, a reactive oxidant; **catalase** protects against H₂O₂ accumulation; **oxoacid** is an organic acid with a carbonyl group; **acetyl-CoA** links fatty-acid breakdown with other metabolic pathways.\n\n\n### Understanding Note — uricase in humans\nThe lecturer’s list includes **urate oxidase (uricase)** as a peroxisomal enzyme across organisms. **Humans lack functional uricase**; do not infer that human peroxisomes convert uric acid to allantoin through this enzyme.\n\n\nThe lecture lists **urate oxidase** among peroxisomal enzymes (PDF page 207). Preserve that list for lecture-based revision. **Scientific clarification:** humans lack functional urate oxidase (uricase), so human purine catabolism generally ends with uric acid rather than conversion to allantoin by this enzyme. This distinction matters when applying the slide to human physiology.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 206–209."},{"id":"OR02","title":"OR02 — Glyoxysomes and Hydrogenosomes","markdown":"# OR02 — Glyoxysomes and Hydrogenosomes\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## Glyoxysomes\n**Glyoxysomes** are specialised plant microbodies, especially important in **germinating oil-rich seeds**. They contain enzymes of the **glyoxylate cycle**, allowing carbon from stored fatty acids to contribute to carbohydrate production.\n\nThe sequence presented by the lecture is: **stored fats → acetyl-CoA → glyoxylate-cycle intermediates → net succinate → pathways contributing to sugar formation**. This supports transport of energy reserves to growing seed tissues. **Scientific clarification:** succinate itself is not the immediate substrate of gluconeogenesis; it must first be converted through intermediary metabolic reactions. The slide's wording about transport to mitochondria and subsequent gluconeogenesis is a simplified pathway overview.\n\n## Hydrogenosomes\nA **hydrogenosome** is a membrane-enclosed energy-producing organelle found in some **anaerobic eukaryotes**, including certain trichomonads, ciliates and fungi. It can release **molecular hydrogen (H₂)** as a by-product of energy metabolism under oxygen-poor conditions. It is related evolutionarily to mitochondria, but its metabolic pathways differ from conventional aerobic mitochondrial respiration.\n\n## Glycosomes: listed but not explained in the slides\nThe lecture names **glycosomes** among microbodies without providing their detailed biochemistry. As supplementary clarification, glycosomes compartmentalise much of glycolysis in some protozoa, notably kinetoplastids; do not confuse them with glycogen granules.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nGlyoxysome—plant seed lipid-to-carbohydrate conversion via glyoxylate cycle; net succinate from acetyl-CoA; hydrogenosome—anaerobic eukaryote organelle releasing H₂; glycosome—named in the lecture but not developed.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Glyoxylate cycle** bypasses carbon-loss steps of the citric-acid cycle; **gluconeogenesis** forms glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors; **anaerobic** means without reliance on oxygen; **H₂** is molecular hydrogen, not hydrogen peroxide.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 210–213."},{"id":"OR03","title":"OR03 — Lysosomes: Structure and Intracellular Digestion","markdown":"# OR03 — Lysosomes: Structure and Intracellular Digestion\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## Lysosomal structure\n**Lysosomes** are single-membrane vesicular organelles containing **acid hydrolases**—enzymes that digest biological macromolecules. The lecture gives approximately **60 hydrolytic enzymes** and an internal **pH reaching approximately 5.0**. A membrane **H⁺-ATPase** uses ATP to pump H⁺ ions inward until the lumen reaches approximately pH 5.0, maintaining the acidic environment described in the slides.\n\n## Formation and delivery of material\nThe slides describe **primary lysosomes** arising from Golgi-derived vesicles, then fusing with material-containing compartments to form **secondary lysosomes**. For revision, know this terminology. **Understanding note:** modern cell biology describes a dynamic endosome–lysosome maturation and fusion system rather than only two rigid lysosome classes.\n\n**Receptor-mediated endocytosis** brings extracellular material into **endosomes**. **Phagocytosis** encloses larger particles in **phagosomes**. Fusion with lysosomal compartments enables breakdown of their contents.\n\n## Heterophagy, autophagy and residual bodies\n- **Heterophagy:** digestion of material originating outside the cell.\n- **Autophagy:** lysosome-dependent breakdown and recycling of the cell's own components, including worn-out organelles such as mitochondria.\n- **Residual body:** compartment containing material that has not been completely digested; such material may persist or be expelled.\n\nDigestion products, such as amino acids and sugars, can be transported out of the lysosome for reuse. The lysosomal limiting membrane is protected from the enzymes it contains and helps retain digestive activity within the compartment.\n\n## Autolysis\n**Autolysis** refers to breakdown of cells or tissues by their own enzymes, particularly after cell death; it should not be confused with regulated autophagy in living cells.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nGolgi/endosomal pathway; acid hydrolases; ~60 enzymes; pH ~5; H⁺-ATPase; primary/secondary lysosome terminology; endosome and phagosome; heterophagy versus autophagy; residual body; autolysis.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Hydrolase** cleaves chemical bonds using water; **endosome** sorts endocytosed material; **phagosome** encloses engulfed particles; **ATPase** hydrolyses ATP to power transport; **autophagy** recycles intracellular components.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 214–218."},{"id":"OR04","title":"OR04 — Lysosomes in Disease: Silicosis and Tay–Sachs","markdown":"# OR04 — Lysosomes in Disease: Silicosis and Tay–Sachs\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## Silicosis: why lysosomal damage matters\nThe lecture links **silicosis** to inhalation of silica particles that are engulfed by lung phagocytes. Crystalline silica can damage phagocytes, including lysosomal membranes, provoking inflammation and cell injury. Persistent inflammation promotes **fibroblast activation and collagen deposition**, causing **pulmonary fibrosis**. Scarred lungs are less compliant, so breathing becomes difficult.\n\nThe slides compare this broad injury-and-fibrosis pattern with **asbestosis** (asbestos fibres) and **black lung disease/coal workers' pneumoconiosis** (coal mine dust). These are distinct diseases with different exposures and mechanisms; they are not synonyms for silicosis.\n\n## Lysosomal storage disorders\nWhen a lysosomal enzyme is absent or severely deficient, its usual substrate may accumulate within cells. Such diseases are called **lysosomal storage disorders**. The consequences depend on the substrate, the affected tissues and the degree of enzyme deficiency.\n\n## Tay–Sachs disease\nThe lecture uses **Tay–Sachs disorder** as an example. **HEXA gene** defects cause deficiency of **β-hexosaminidase A**, impairing degradation of **GM2 ganglioside**, a glycolipid especially important in neurons. Accumulation leads to progressive neurological dysfunction.\n\nClassic infantile Tay–Sachs commonly presents with developmental regression, exaggerated startle responses, weakness, later seizures and loss of vision; severe disease can cause early childhood death. The slide mentions Eastern European ancestry; **important clarification:** risk is increased in some populations, including Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry, but the condition can affect people of any ancestry. **Diagnosis** is based on enzyme testing and/or molecular genetic testing; a general chromosome count test is not sufficient.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nSilica uptake → phagocyte injury/inflammation → fibroblasts and collagen → reduced lung elasticity; silicosis versus asbestosis versus coal dust disease; enzyme deficiency → lysosomal accumulation; Tay–Sachs → GM2 buildup and neurological decline.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Fibrosis** is excess scar-like connective tissue; **fibroblast** produces extracellular matrix including collagen; **lysosomal storage disorder** involves impaired substrate breakdown; **GM2 ganglioside** is a neuronal membrane glycolipid; **β-hexosaminidase A** is the deficient enzyme in Tay–Sachs.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 219–226."},{"id":"OR05","title":"OR05 — Nucleus: Envelope, Pores and Genetic Instructions","markdown":"# OR05 — Nucleus: Envelope, Pores and Genetic Instructions\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## The nuclear envelope\nThe **nucleus** is a major compartment of eukaryotic cells containing most of their DNA. Its **nuclear envelope** has **two membranes** separated by a **perinuclear space**. The outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the **rough endoplasmic reticulum**. **Nuclear pore complexes** span the envelope and regulate exchange between nucleoplasm and cytoplasm.\n\nThe lecture describes an **eightfold-symmetric** protein arrangement surrounding nuclear pores, and uses the term *porosome* for the pore and annulus. **Terminology clarification:** *nuclear pore complex (NPC)* is the standard modern term; *porosome* is also used for a different secretory structure in some literature, so avoid treating it as an unambiguous synonym.\n\n## Selective nuclear transport\nThe pore does not simply act as a hole that lets all small molecules pass and blocks all large ones. Small molecules may diffuse, while larger cargo can be transported selectively using nuclear transport receptors and targeting signals. Proteins destined for the nucleus are generally synthesised on cytosolic ribosomes and carry **nuclear localisation signals**.\n\n## DNA, chromosomes and chromatin\nThe slide states **46 chromosomes** in human cells. More precisely, **typical human diploid somatic cells have 46 chromosomes (23 pairs)**; gametes have 23, and some specialised cells differ. **Chromatin** is DNA associated with proteins, including **histones**. A **nucleosome** is a repeating structural unit of chromatin, consisting of DNA wrapped around a histone core. During cell division, chromatin becomes highly condensed into recognisable chromosomes.\n\n## From gene to protein\n**Transcription** copies genetic information from DNA into RNA, including messenger RNA (**mRNA**). **Translation** reads mRNA at ribosomes to assemble amino acids into a protein. A gene does not always encode a protein; some genes encode functional RNAs. The slide's DNA → mRNA → protein sequence remains the central example.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nDouble nuclear envelope; perinuclear space; outer membrane continuous with rough ER; nuclear pore complex and selective transport; nuclear localisation signals; human somatic-cell chromosome number; chromatin, histones, nucleosomes; transcription and translation.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Nucleoplasm** is the material within the nucleus; **chromatin** is DNA plus associated proteins; **histone** helps package DNA; **nucleosome** is the chromatin repeat unit; **transcription** makes RNA; **translation** makes protein.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 227–234."},{"id":"OR06","title":"OR06 — Nucleolus, Chromatin and Chromosomes","markdown":"# OR06 — Nucleolus, Chromatin and Chromosomes\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## The nucleolus\nThe **nucleolus** is a prominent, **membrane-less** region of the nucleus where **ribosomal RNA (rRNA)** is produced and **ribosomal subunits** are assembled. Ribosomal proteins are made in the cytoplasm and imported into the nucleus; they combine with rRNA to form subunits that are later exported.\n\nThe nucleolus commonly appears as a dark-staining region under a light microscope. It forms around **nucleolar organiser regions (NORs)**, chromosome regions containing rRNA genes. During typical open mitosis, nucleolar organisation disassembles and is re-established afterward. The slide mentions up to four nucleoli and a fixed number for each species; **clarification:** nucleolar number varies with cell type, activity and cell-cycle state, so a universal fixed count should not be memorised as a rule.\n\nThe slides also mention possible roles of the nucleolus in **cellular ageing/senescence**. This is a research connection, not its principal textbook function.\n\n## Chromatin versus chromosomes\n**Chromatin** refers to DNA and associated proteins in the nucleus. **Chromosomes** are discrete DNA–protein structures; they are especially visible when chromatin condenses during division. Chromatin does not disappear outside mitosis, and chromosomes are not newly created only when a cell divides.\n\n**Nucleosomes** provide a basic level of DNA packaging. **Histones** are basic proteins that associate with negatively charged DNA. **Euchromatin** is generally less condensed and more accessible; **heterochromatin** is generally more condensed, though activity depends on context. These latter terms support interpretation of the diagram slides.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nNo membrane around nucleolus; rRNA synthesis; ribosomal subunit assembly; NORs; changes during division; chromatin = DNA + protein; nucleosomes; chromosomes become highly condensed during division.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**rRNA** is a structural/catalytic component of ribosomes; **ribosomal subunit** combines rRNA and proteins; **NOR** contains rRNA gene clusters; **senescence** is a durable loss of cell-division capacity; **euchromatin/heterochromatin** refer to comparatively open/compact chromatin.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 235–240."},{"id":"OR07","title":"OR07 — Centrosome and Centrioles","markdown":"# OR07 — Centrosome and Centrioles\n\n\n**Learning Mission:** Explain the defining structure, major functions, and key distinctions in this lesson.\n\n## Centrosome: the microtubule-organising centre\nIn many animal cells, the **centrosome** lies near the nucleus and serves as a principal **microtubule-organising centre (MTOC)**. Microtubules radiate from the surrounding **pericentriolar material (PCM)**, which contains proteins needed to nucleate and anchor microtubules.\n\n## Centrioles\nA typical animal-cell centrosome contains **two centrioles**, often arranged approximately **at right angles**. Each centriole has **nine sets of microtubule triplets** arranged in a ring (**9 × 3**). **Do not confuse** this with the **9 + 2** arrangement of microtubule doublets in many motile cilia and flagella.\n\n## Polarity and dynamic growth\nA microtubule has a **minus end** and a **plus end**. In the radial arrays described by the lecture, the minus end is usually anchored near the centrosome, while the plus end extends toward the cell periphery and can rapidly grow or shrink. This behaviour is called **dynamic instability**.\n\n## Cell division and cilia\nCentrosomes contribute to organisation of the **mitotic spindle**, which separates chromosomes during cell division. Centrioles duplicate once per cell cycle under normal regulation. Centrioles can also give rise to **basal bodies**, which organise cilia and flagella. **Clarification:** not all eukaryotes possess centrioles, and cells can organise microtubules without a canonical centrosome.\n\n## Slide Essentials\nNear nucleus; pericentriolar material; two centrioles at ~90°; nine triplets per centriole; microtubule plus end outward; mitotic spindle; centriole duplication; basal bodies and cilia/flagella.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**MTOC** organises microtubules; **PCM** surrounds centrioles and nucleates microtubules; **microtubule polarity** distinguishes plus/minus ends; **dynamic instability** is alternating growth/shrinkage; **basal body** anchors and organises a cilium.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 Cell Structure, PDF pages 241–244."},{"id":"CM01","title":"CM01 — Cytosol and the phospholipid bilayer","markdown":"# CM01 — Cytosol and the phospholipid bilayer\n\n\n## Learning mission\nDistinguish cytoplasm from cytosol, explain why membranes form bilayers, and connect phospholipid structure to membrane function.\n\n## Core lesson\n**Cytoplasm** is the cellular region outside the nucleus; it includes cytosol and cytoplasmic organelles. **Cytosol** is its aqueous phase, excluding membrane-enclosed organelles. It contains dissolved ions, simple sugars, proteins and other molecules, and inclusions such as glycogen granules and lipid droplets. Many metabolic reactions occur in the cytosol. The slides describe cytosol as about **55% of cell volume** and **75–90% water**; these are approximate lecture figures, not universal constants.\n\nThe **plasma membrane** separates the cell interior from its surroundings. Organelles also have specialized **biomembranes**, whose distinct proteins help them perform different functions. Membranes are not rigid walls: they are dynamic arrangements of lipids and proteins.\n\nA **phospholipid** is **amphipathic**: its polar, hydrophilic head interacts with water while its nonpolar, hydrophobic fatty-acid tails avoid water. In a bilayer, the heads face the aqueous exterior and interior, and the tails face one another. This arrangement creates a selective barrier.\n\nIn a common phosphoglyceride, glycerol bears two fatty-acid chains and a phosphate-containing head group. The **fluid mosaic model** describes proteins embedded in or associated with a mobile lipid bilayer. Proteins provide specialized functions; they are not merely decorative parts of the mosaic.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Cytosol: aqueous cytoplasmic phase; includes dissolved solutes and inclusions (PDF 285–286).\n- Plasma membrane: phospholipid bilayer plus associated lipids and proteins (287–290).\n- Hydrophilic heads face water; hydrophobic tails form the interior (291–294).\n- Amphipathic and fluid mosaic are examinable terms.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Amphipathic** → contains hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions. **Bilayer** → self-assembled arrangement in water. **Selective barrier** → permeability depends on solute properties and transport proteins. **Biomembrane** → shared structural principle, organelle-specific functions.\n\n\n### Understanding Note — membrane fluidity\nCholesterol generally **restrains excessive phospholipid movement at higher temperatures** while **disrupting tight phospholipid packing at lower temperatures**. Its effect is temperature- and membrane-composition-dependent; avoid describing it as invariably increasing or decreasing fluidity.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 PDF pp. 285–294."},{"id":"CM02","title":"CM02 — Cholesterol and membrane fluidity","markdown":"# CM02 — Cholesterol and membrane fluidity\n\n\n## Learning mission\nExplain how membrane lipids move and how temperature, fatty-acid saturation and cholesterol affect fluidity.\n\n## Core lesson\nMembrane **fluidity** means that lipids and some proteins can move within the plane of the bilayer. **Lateral diffusion** is relatively common. **Transverse movement (flip-flop)** between leaflets is much less common without enzymes that facilitate lipid transfer.\n\nThe fatty-acid tails strongly affect packing. **Unsaturated tails** commonly contain cis double bonds that create bends, reducing tight packing and helping maintain fluidity. **Saturated tails** tend to pack more closely. Cooling generally reduces lipid movement and may cause a membrane to shift into a more ordered state; the transition depends on lipid composition.\n\n**Cholesterol** is an important component of animal-cell membranes. At relatively warm temperatures it restrains phospholipid movement; at low temperatures it disrupts close packing and can help prevent abrupt solidification. Thus cholesterol buffers membrane fluidity rather than simply making membranes 'more fluid' in every condition.\n\nThe slide illustration contrasts frequent lateral movement with very rare spontaneous flip-flop, giving examples of approximately **10⁷ lateral movements per second** and **once per month** for flip-flop. Treat these as illustrative slide estimates, not fixed rates for every lipid and membrane.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Lipids and some proteins move laterally (PDF 302).\n- Unsaturated tails generally support greater fluidity than saturated tails (303–304).\n- Cholesterol restrains movement at warm temperatures but reduces close packing when cool (305).\n- Lateral diffusion ≠ transverse flip-flop (306).\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Unsaturation** → bends in acyl chains → looser packing. **Temperature** → affects lipid motion. **Cholesterol** → modulates packing. **Flip-flop** → changes membrane leaflet composition and may require enzymes.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 PDF pp. 295–306."},{"id":"CM03","title":"CM03 — Membrane lipids, proteins and glycocalyx","markdown":"# CM03 — Membrane lipids, proteins and glycocalyx\n\n\n## Learning mission\nClassify membrane components and explain how proteins support transport, signaling and cell recognition.\n\n## Core lesson\nThe phospholipid bilayer contains several types of lipids and proteins. The lecture gives **phospholipids as 75%** and **glycolipids as 5%** of membrane lipids in its representative composition. These are slide-specific proportions; membrane composition differs between cell types and organelles.\n\n**Glycolipids** carry carbohydrate groups that face the extracellular side of the plasma membrane. **Glycoproteins** are proteins with attached carbohydrate chains. Together with other surface carbohydrates, they contribute to the **glycocalyx**, a carbohydrate-rich external coat involved in recognition, adhesion and protection.\n\n**Integral membrane proteins** associate strongly with the lipid bilayer; many contain hydrophobic segments buried among lipid tails. **Transmembrane proteins** span the bilayer. **Peripheral proteins** bind more loosely to membrane surfaces or other proteins and can often be detached without disrupting the lipid bilayer.\n\nMembrane proteins perform several functions. **Channels** form selective pathways for particular solutes. **Transporters** bind substrates and undergo conformational changes to move them across. **Receptors** bind signals and initiate responses. Other membrane proteins participate in recognition, attachment, enzymes and anchoring the cytoskeleton. A receptor need not itself transport its ligand.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Representative slide figures: phospholipids 75%, glycolipids 5% (PDF 308, 311); not universal values.\n- Integral versus transmembrane versus peripheral proteins (312–314).\n- Extracellular carbohydrate groups and glycocalyx (311, 313).\n- Channels, transporters and receptors (315–317).\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Integral** describes tight membrane association; **transmembrane** specifies spanning both sides. **Glycocalyx** links surface carbohydrates to recognition. **Channel** provides a pore; **carrier/transporter** binds and changes conformation; **receptor** detects a signal.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 PDF pp. 307–317."},{"id":"CM04","title":"CM04 — Selective permeability and electrochemical gradients","markdown":"# CM04 — Selective permeability and electrochemical gradients\n\n\n## Learning mission\nPredict which substances cross membranes readily and explain the combined effects of concentration and voltage gradients.\n\n## Core lesson\nA membrane is **selectively permeable**: different substances cross it at different rates. Small, nonpolar molecules such as **oxygen and carbon dioxide** usually cross the lipid bilayer relatively readily. Polar molecules and ions generally require appropriate transport proteins for efficient passage. Water can cross the lipid bilayer to some extent, but **aquaporins** greatly increase water permeability in many cells. Large cargo may be moved by **vesicular transport** rather than diffusing through the bilayer.\n\nA **concentration gradient** is a difference in solute concentration across the membrane. An **electrical gradient** results from a voltage difference. For an ion, these two influences combine into an **electrochemical gradient**. The concentration component favors movement from higher to lower concentration; the electrical component attracts opposite charges and repels like charges. The net direction depends on both, not concentration alone.\n\nThe slides use representative animal-cell gradients: **Na⁺** is generally higher outside and **K⁺** higher inside. They also list more O₂ outside and CO₂ inside; these gas patterns are context-dependent and should not be treated as universal across all tissues or conditions. Maintaining ion gradients often requires active transport, including the sodium–potassium pump.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Nonpolar uncharged molecules such as O₂ and CO₂ can cross the lipid core (PDF 318).\n- Specific transmembrane pathways facilitate passage of ions and many polar solutes (318).\n- Vesicular transport can move larger cargo (318).\n- Concentration gradient + electrical gradient = electrochemical driving forces (319–320).\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Selective permeability** determines available routes. **Channels** permit facilitated passage. **Pumps** can build gradients using energy. **Electrochemical gradient** explains ion movement. **Membrane potential** is the electrical component of the driving force.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 PDF pp. 318–321."},{"id":"CY01","title":"CY01 — Cytoskeleton: architecture and movement","markdown":"# CY01 — Cytoskeleton: architecture and movement\n\n## Learning mission\nExplain how a dynamic filament network maintains cell shape, positions organelles and supports transport and movement.\n\n## Core lesson\nThe **cytoskeleton** is a three-dimensional, dynamic network of protein filaments and associated proteins extending through the cytoplasm. It is not a rigid skeleton: its filaments assemble, disassemble and reorganize as cellular demands change. This enables a cell to maintain a recognizable shape while also changing shape, transporting cargo and sometimes moving as a whole.\n\nIts two broad roles are **structural support** and **transport**. The lecturer likens the filament framework to a **geodesic dome** that balances opposing forces; unlike a rigid dome, cytoskeletal filaments can dismantle in one region and reassemble in another. This allows the cell to change shape while retaining mechanical support. Structural support includes mechanical stability, anchoring and positioning organelles, and organizing specialized cellular regions. Transport includes movement of vesicles and organelles within a cell; cytoskeletal systems also contribute to whole-cell movement. Filaments can be linked to one another or other structures, producing a three-dimensional framework.\n\nThe lecturer also lists movement of **cilia, flagella and sperm**, **muscle contraction**, and cytoskeletal participation in **cell division** among its functions. These are distinct examples of cytoskeleton-dependent movement.\n\n**Why red blood cells are not spherical:** the membrane-associated cytoskeleton helps maintain their characteristic flexible biconcave shape. The cytoskeleton is also essential to long, specialized cells such as neurons, which must maintain extended axons while transporting material along them.\n\nThe cytoskeleton's organization is constantly remodeled: support and movement are coordinated, not opposing tasks. All three major filament classes contribute to cell architecture, but their mechanical and dynamic properties differ.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Cytoskeleton: protein-filament network within the cytoplasm, with associated motor and linking proteins.\n- Functions: structural support; transport; cell shape change and movement; positioning organelles.\n- Highly dynamic, unlike the relatively fixed analogy of a human skeleton.\n- Examples: red blood cell shape; long neuronal axons; movement of cellular cargo.\n- Lecturer analogy: filaments balance opposing forces like a **geodesic dome**.\n- Movement examples: cilia, flagella, sperm, muscle contraction and cell division.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Filament polymerization** = assembling protein subunits into a filament; **depolymerization** = disassembly. **Organelle positioning** links cytoskeletal structure to intracellular specialization. **Membrane skeleton** links filament networks to red-cell shape.","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CY02","title":"CY02 — Three filament systems and motor proteins","markdown":"# CY02 — Three filament systems and motor proteins\n\n## Learning mission\nDistinguish the three major filament classes by composition, approximate diameter and functions, and identify the principal motor-protein families.\n\n## Core lesson\nThe lecture separates **shapers** (protein filaments) from **movers** (motor proteins). The three filament classes are **microtubules**, **intermediate filaments**, and **actin filaments (microfilaments)**. Each is assembled from protein subunits, but their sizes and functions differ.\n\n| Filament | Lecture diameter | Main protein building blocks | Key roles |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Microtubules | ~25 nm outer diameter | α/β-tubulin | Long-distance intracellular transport, spindle organization, cilia and flagella |\n| Intermediate filaments | ~10 nm | Tissue-specific proteins such as keratins and neurofilament proteins | Mechanical strength; resistance to stretching |\n| Actin filaments | ~8 nm | Actin | Shape changes, cell cortex, contraction, cytokinesis and cell movement |\n\n**Motor proteins** convert chemical energy from ATP into mechanical work. The lecture names **kinesin, dynein and myosin**. Kinesins and dyneins commonly move cargo along microtubules; myosins interact with actin. Specific motors have distinct directions and tasks, so not every kinesin or dynein should be described as doing exactly the same job.\n\nThe lecture also distinguishes the roles of the three filament classes: **actin** in membrane contraction, muscle cells and cytokinesis; **intermediate filaments** in resisting mechanical stress; and **microtubules** in cytoplasmic transport, axoneme movement and chromosome movement.\n\nIntermediate filaments are especially important in tissues exposed to mechanical stress. The lecture associates intermediate-filament abnormalities with **epidermolysis bullosa simplex** (keratin defects and skin blistering), **Mallory bodies** in alcoholic liver cirrhosis (keratin-containing inclusions), and **Alzheimer’s disease** in a slide discussing changes in neuronal filaments. Scientific clarification: Alzheimer’s disease cannot be attributed simply to altered neurofilaments; multiple pathological mechanisms are involved, including amyloid and tau-related processes. These are associations, not proof that a single filament change alone explains all cases of a complex disease.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Three shapers: microtubules, intermediate filaments, actin filaments.\n- Three movers: kinesin, dynein, myosin.\n- Lecture diameters: 25 nm, 10 nm, 8 nm respectively.\n- Keratin filament defects can weaken epidermal cells and cause blistering.\n- The lecturer lists **epidermolysis bullosa simplex**, **Alzheimer’s disease**, and **alcoholic liver cirrhosis (Mallory bodies)** under intermediate-filament disorders. This is a lecture classification; the disease mechanisms require the clarification in this lesson.\n- Mallory bodies contain keratin intermediate filaments; their presence is not exclusive to alcoholic liver disease.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Microfilament** = actin filament. **Motor protein** = ATP-powered protein that generates movement on cytoskeletal tracks. **Mechanical resilience** = ability to withstand physical stress without cell rupture.","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CY03","title":"CY03 — Microtubule structure, polarity and dynamic instability","markdown":"# CY03 — Microtubule structure, polarity and dynamic instability\n\n## Learning mission\nDescribe the structure of a microtubule and explain how polarity, assembly and disassembly support intracellular transport.\n\n## Core lesson\nThe lecturer describes microtubules as **stiff but flexible** and notes that human axons can extend **more than one metre**; microtubules serve as transport tracks over these distances.\n\nA **microtubule** is a hollow, roughly **25 nm**-wide cylinder built from **α- and β-tubulin** subunits. In a typical cytoplasmic microtubule, **13 protofilaments** align around the wall of the tube. A protofilament is a linear chain of repeating α/β-tubulin dimers.\n\nBecause α- and β-tubulin are arranged consistently, microtubules have distinct **plus (+)** and **minus (−)** ends. These labels describe structural polarity, not electrical charge. The plus end often changes length more rapidly, although behavior depends on cellular regulation. Assembly requires sufficient available tubulin and appropriate conditions; the lecture highlights a **critical concentration** of tubulin.\n\n**Dynamic instability** is the switching of individual microtubule ends between growth and shrinkage. Tubulin bound to GTP is incorporated into a growing microtubule; GTP hydrolysis influences stability. Loss of a stabilizing GTP-rich end region can trigger rapid shortening (catastrophe), while renewed growth is called rescue. This explains why microtubules can maintain tracks yet rapidly reorganize during mitosis.\n\nA **microtubule-organizing centre (MTOC)** helps nucleate and organize microtubules. In many animal cells, the centrosome is a major MTOC. Microtubules also provide tracks for long-distance axonal transport.\n\n**Microtubules and Alzheimer's disease:** the lecture describes tau as a microtubule-associated protein (MAP), and relates abnormal tau aggregation and microtubule disruption to impaired neuronal transport. Important qualification: Alzheimer's disease is multifactorial; tau pathology is one major component, not a complete single-cause explanation.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Microtubule: stiff but flexible hollow cylinder, ~25 nm diameter, usually 13 protofilaments.\n- Axonal transport may extend along axons more than one metre long.\n- Building block: α/β-tubulin dimer.\n- Ends: structurally distinct plus and minus ends.\n- Dynamic instability: alternating growth and shrinkage; important for spindle rearrangement.\n- Tau is a microtubule-associated protein; altered tau is relevant to neuronal disease.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Protofilament** = linear tubulin polymer; **polarity** = directional asymmetry of filament structure; **MTOC** = site organizing microtubule growth; **tau** = neuronal MAP that interacts with microtubules.","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CY04","title":"CY04 — Microtubules in mitosis and medicine","markdown":"# CY04 — Microtubules in mitosis and medicine\n\n## Learning mission\nExplain why disrupting microtubule dynamics can prevent cell division and distinguish stabilizing from destabilizing drugs.\n\n## Core lesson\nDuring **mitosis**, microtubules form the spindle apparatus. Spindle microtubules help align chromosomes and move them toward opposite poles. These activities require controlled assembly, disassembly and motor-driven movement. A cell may fail to complete division if its spindle cannot reorganize correctly.\n\nThis makes microtubules an important target in cancer treatment. The lecture distinguishes two broad pharmacological strategies:\n\n1. **Prevent microtubule assembly or promote disassembly.** This reduces functional spindle formation. The lecture names **colchicine, nocodazole and vincristine** as drugs that inhibit assembly or favor depolymerization.\n2. **Stabilize microtubules and inhibit disassembly.** This prevents the normal rearrangements required for spindle function. **Taxol (paclitaxel)** is the lecture's example.\n\nBoth excessive stabilization and excessive destabilization can interfere with mitosis. The goal is not simply to destroy every microtubule, since normal cells also need them. Clinical usefulness depends on therapeutic benefit, selectivity, dosing and adverse effects. The lecturer specifically emphasizes the challenge of limiting damage to **non-cancerous cells** and achieving **safe, effective delivery** with minimal general side effects.\n\n**Clinical precision:** vincristine and paclitaxel are established anticancer drugs. Colchicine and nocodazole are useful mechanistic examples in the lecture, but they should not all be presented as interchangeable routine cancer therapies.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Mitotic spindle: microtubule system involved in chromosome alignment and segregation.\n- **Taxol/paclitaxel**: stabilizes microtubules and inhibits normal disassembly.\n- **Vincristine**: interferes with microtubule assembly; used in cancer chemotherapy.\n- **Colchicine and nocodazole**: inhibit microtubule polymerization (important lecture examples).\n- Both blocking polymerization and blocking depolymerization can disrupt cell division.\n- The lecture emphasizes effects on healthy cells, safe delivery, and systemic side effects.\n\n## Concept Connections\n**Polymerization** = building a filament; **depolymerization** = breaking it down; **spindle** = microtubule-based chromosome segregation apparatus; **antimitotic agent** = agent that disrupts mitosis.","references":[],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CE01","title":"CE01 — Cell Disruption and Homogenisation","markdown":"# CE01 — Cell Disruption and Homogenisation\n**Lecture source:** BIOL 151 PDF pages 322–340 (printed slide numbers 329–347). **Academic status:** corrected against extractable slide text; image-only slide verification remains open.\n\n## Cell fractionation: purpose and sequence\nCell fractionation investigates functions of individual cell compartments by disrupting cells and separating their components. The lecturer presents three steps: **(1) disruption or homogenisation**, normally in a supportive **iso-osmotic medium**, producing a **homogenate**; **(2) separation of cell fragments and organelles** for individual study; and **(3) biochemical analysis** of the separated fractions. An iso-osmotic medium helps preserve the structure of organelles, although lysis methods may intentionally use hypotonic conditions.\n\n## Cell homogenisation and lysis\nCell lysis is an early step in cell fractionation and protein purification. The lecture distinguishes physical methods from detergent-based disruption. Choose the method according to the cell type, volume and whether intact organelles or soluble contents are required.\n\n### Mechanical disruption — Waring blender and Polytron \nRotating blades grind and disperse relatively large amounts of complex tissues such as liver and muscle. The **Waring blender** resembles a household blender. The **Polytron** draws tissue into a long shaft containing rotating blades. These are not interchangeable with the gentle methods used to isolate fragile organelles.\n\n### Liquid homogenisation — Dounce, Potter–Elvehjem and French press \nLiquid homogenisation forces a suspension through a narrow gap, shearing membranes. The **Dounce homogeniser** has a rounded glass pestle moved manually inside a glass tube. The **Potter–Elvehjem homogeniser** is another pestle-and-tube design. **Number and speed of strokes** affect disruption efficiency. A **French press** uses a piston to apply high pressure and force material through a small opening. The lecturer specifies **40–250 mL** sample volumes and states that **two passes** are generally sufficient in the example discussed. These are slide-specific descriptions, not universal specifications for every French press.\n\n### Sonication \nA vibrating probe delivers pulsed, high-frequency sound waves to the suspension. Cavitation involves microscopic vapour bubbles forming and collapsing, generating disruptive forces. To limit heat damage, the lecturer recommends **multiple short bursts with the sample in an ice bath**. The lecture describes sonication as best suited to volumes **below 100 mL**; this is a teaching guideline, not an absolute instrument limit.\n\n### Freeze–thaw \nCells are frozen (for example, in a dry-ice/ethanol bath or freezer) and thawed at room temperature or **37 °C**. Ice formation and associated physical stress disrupt membranes. **Repeated cycles** are generally needed. The lecture describes uses in bacterial and mammalian cells, including recovery of bacterial cytoplasmic recombinant proteins. Scientific clarification: ice-crystal injury and osmotic/mechanical stresses contribute; the process is not simply cells swelling because ice expands.\n\n### Mortar and pestle \nFor plant cells, tissue may be frozen in **liquid nitrogen** and manually ground. Tough cell walls containing **cellulose and other polysaccharides** make this especially useful for accessing plant proteins and DNA.\n\n## Additives and facilitators \n- **Hypotonic buffer:** promotes swelling and easier disruption, but differs from the iso-osmotic medium used to protect isolated organelles.\n- **Cell-wall digestion:** the lecturer specifies **200 µg/mL** for an agent used to digest polysaccharide components of yeast and bacterial walls; the extracted slide text does **not identify the agent**, so its name must not be guessed from this source.\n- **Glass beads:** can assist mechanical disruption.\n- **Nucleases:** nucleic-acid release raises lysate viscosity. The lecture lists **DNase 25–50 µg/mL** and **RNase 50 µg/mL** to reduce viscosity. It notes that nuclease treatment may not be needed after sonication because sonication can shear chromosomes; this depends on protocol.\n- **Protease inhibitors:** help limit proteolytic degradation during sample handling.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n**Waring blender; Polytron; Dounce; Potter–Elvehjem; French press; sonication; freeze–thaw; mortar and pestle; hypotonic versus iso-osmotic media; glass beads; DNase; RNase; protease inhibitors.** Remember the lecturer's **40–250 mL, \u003c100 mL, 37 °C, 200 µg/mL, DNase 25–50 µg/mL and RNase 50 µg/mL** where specifically taught.\n\n## Concept Connections\nA disruption method changes which cellular structures remain intact. Iso-osmotic support helps preserve organelles for fractionation; hypotonic lysis or detergents can deliberately destroy membranes. Subsequent centrifugation enriches components, and biochemical assays help identify what is in each fraction.\n\n## Short concept checks\n1. Name the three stages of cell fractionation. **Disruption, separation, biochemical analysis.**\n2. Distinguish the Waring blender from the Polytron. **Both use rotating blades; the Polytron draws tissue into a bladed shaft.**\n3. Which two variables affect Dounce/Potter homogenisation? **Stroke number and speed.**\n4. Why use short sonication bursts on ice? **To reduce excessive sample heating.**\n5. Which sample preparation is used before mortar-and-pestle grinding of plant tissue? **Freezing in liquid nitrogen.**\n6. Why can DNase and RNase be added? **To reduce viscosity from released nucleic acids.**","references":["(p.324)","(pp.325–330)","(pp.331–334)","(pp.335–336)","(p.337)","(pp.338–339)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CE02","title":"CE02 — Centrifugation and Differential Fractionation","markdown":"# CE02 — Centrifugation and Differential Fractionation\n**Lecturer source:** PDF pp. 341–352. **Editorial status:** source-reconciled; diagram approval pending.\n\n## Centrifugation after homogenisation\nA **homogenate** is the disrupted cell preparation. The lecture identifies centrifugation as the most widely used method to separate its intracellular components. A centrifugal field accelerates sedimentation; the sedimentation rate depends on **size, density and shape** . The lecture names two common techniques for separating bacterial components: **differential centrifugation** and **buoyant-density centrifugation** . These methods are also used more broadly in cell biology.\n\n## Differential centrifugation\nRepeatedly centrifuge the homogenate at **increasing centrifugal force**. A **pellet** collects at the bottom; the **supernatant** remains above it. Decant or transfer the supernatant and centrifuge it again to obtain successively smaller fractions . The lecturer emphasizes that separation is **primarily by particle size**, with preparations that are only **partially pure**. Particle density and shape also affect sedimentation.\n\n### Exact lecture example \n1. **1,000 × g for 10 minutes:** unbroken cells and heavy nuclei pellet.\n2. **10,000 × g for 20 minutes:** the subsequent supernatant yields a pellet enriched in **mitochondria, lysosomes and microbodies**.\n\nThese are the lecture's example conditions, **not universal rotor settings**. Fractions may contain contaminants. The lecturer describes washing pellets by resuspension in **isotonic solvents** and re-pelleting to remove smaller contaminants . Differential fractionation is a **preliminary purification step** before density-gradient separation.\n\n## Scientific understanding note\nRelative centrifugal force (×g) differs from revolutions per minute (rpm); rotor radius matters when converting between them. This is useful background, not a replacement for the lecture's numeric examples.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- **Differential** and **buoyant-density** centrifugation are the two named methods.\n- Preserve the example **1,000 × g/10 min** and **10,000 × g/20 min** and the organelles pelleted.\n- Pellet versus supernatant; repeated faster spins; **partial purity**; isotonic washing.\n\n## Concept Connections\nHomogenisation releases organelles → differential centrifugation enriches organelle fractions → density gradients further purify them → biochemical markers establish fraction identity.","references":["(p. 342)","(p. 341)","(pp. 345–348)","(p. 349)","(p. 350)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CE03","title":"CE03 — Density Gradients, Rate-Zonal and Isopycnic Separation","markdown":"# CE03 — Density Gradients, Rate-Zonal and Isopycnic Separation\n**Lecturer source:** PDF pp. 353–365. **Editorial status:** source-reconciled; diagrams pending.\n\n## Density-gradient (buoyant-density) centrifugation\nThe lecturer describes density gradients as a preferred method for purifying subcellular organelles and macromolecules. A medium such as **sucrose** is arranged with **the densest layer at the bottom** and the lightest at the top. Gradients can be **discontinuous or continuous** . Two separation categories are **rate-zonal (size)** and **isopycnic (density)** .\n\n### Lecturer's sucrose-gradient example \nThe slides refer to **viruses with densities of 1.1–1.2 g/cm³** in a sucrose gradient. Components with higher buoyant densities band lower in the gradient than less-dense components. **Understanding note:** the slide's printed unit is incomplete in text extraction (g/cm); density is conventionally g/cm³. The phrase that denser particles 'move fastest' is a simplification: movement depends on sedimentation properties and gradient conditions.\n\n## Rate-zonal separation\nA sample begins as a zone, usually near the top. Particles separate mainly by **size/mass and sedimentation rate**, rather than equilibrium buoyant density. The lecturer gives **endosomes** and **antibody classes** as examples; antibody classes may have similar densities but different masses . **Understanding note:** sedimentation also depends on shape; a run must be stopped before bands all pellet.\n\n## Isopycnic separation\nParticles move until the surrounding medium matches their **buoyant density**; at approximate equilibrium, further centrifugation time does not substantially change band position . The lecturer's example is **nucleic-acid separation in a CsCl (caesium chloride) gradient** . Keep distinct the **rate-zonal size/mass** mechanism and **isopycnic density** mechanism.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- **Continuous/discontinuous sucrose gradients**, dense bottom/light top.\n- **Rate-zonal:** size/mass; endosomes; antibody classes.\n- **Isopycnic:** equilibrium density; **CsCl nucleic acids**.\n- Lecture example: viruses **1.1–1.2 g/cm³** (unit clarified).\n\n## Concept Connections\nDifferential centrifugation gives partially pure pellets; gradient methods resolve components further. Rate-zonal separates by how fast material travels; isopycnic by where buoyant density matches.","references":["(p. 353)","(p. 359)","(p. 354)","(pp. 359–361)","(pp. 362–363)","(p. 363)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"CE04","title":"CE04 — Analytical Ultracentrifugation and Centrifuge Examples","markdown":"# CE04 — Analytical Ultracentrifugation and Centrifuge Examples\n**Lecturer source:** PDF pp. 366–370. **Editorial status:** source-reconciled; instrument images pending.\n\n## Analytical ultracentrifugation\nThe lecture calls **analytical ultracentrifugation** a classical biochemistry and molecular-biology method with broad applications. It describes the method as a **primary technique requiring no comparison standards** . Sedimentation measurements can investigate solution behaviour across different **solute concentrations and solvents** .\n\nThe lecture describes it as a further refinement of preparative centrifuges, applicable to small molecules and macromolecules including **ribosomes and proteins** . In its specific example, the sample is mixed with **sucrose or caesium chloride medium** in a **quartz centrifuge cell** and spun at forces **up to 500,000 × g**. Sedimentation creates a boundary between clearer solvent above and sample-containing solution below .\n\n## Analytical versus preparative\n**Preparative** work aims to recover fractions for subsequent use. **Analytical** work follows sedimentation behaviour to characterize molecules. **Understanding note:** analytical ultracentrifugation commonly measures sedimentation velocity/equilibrium optically; it should not be understood simply as a way of obtaining pure samples. The lecture's boundary description illustrates sedimentation rather than guaranteeing complete purification.\n\n## Equipment illustrated\nThe final lecture pages label a **Centriflex Centrifuge**  and a **Gilson GmCLab Mini Centrifuge** . Retain those names in captions if their photographs are used.\n\n## Additional understanding note\nA **sedimentation coefficient** may be expressed in Svedberg units (S) and depends on buoyancy and friction as well as mass. This explanation extends the slide text; it is not an explicit statement on pp. 366–370.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Primary technique requiring no comparison standards (lecturer's phrasing).\n- **Ribosomes and proteins**, **quartz cell**, **sucrose/CsCl**, **up to 500,000 × g**.\n- Named instrument photographs: **Centriflex** and **Gilson GmCLab Mini**.\n\n## Concept Connections\nDifferential and density-gradient centrifugation prepare fractions; analytical ultracentrifugation examines sedimentation properties of dissolved macromolecules.","references":["(p. 366)","(p. 367)","(p. 368)","(p. 369)","(p. 370)"],"attribution":"Supplied Cell Structure teaching material"},{"id":"BI01","title":"BI01 — Biochemical analysis and spectrophotometry","markdown":"# BI01 — Biochemical analysis and spectrophotometry\n\n## Core lesson\n\n### From fractions to function\nOnce organelles or cell fractions are isolated, biochemical assays help characterize their activity. The slides identify spectrophotometry, autoradiography and chromatography as example approaches.\n\n### Light absorption\nSpectrophotometry measures light transmitted through or absorbed by a sample at selected wavelengths. Absorbance is defined as A = log10(I0/I), where I0 is incident intensity and I is transmitted intensity.\n\n### Beer–Lambert relationship\nFor suitable dilute solutions, A = εlc, with molar absorptivity ε, path length l and concentration c. Concentration can be estimated from a calibration curve when the assay behaves linearly.\n\n### Limitations and controls\nA blank accounts for solvent and reagent background. Scattering, interfering pigments, incorrect wavelength, dirty cuvettes or excessively concentrated samples can distort readings. Colour intensity does not universally reveal the identity of an unknown compound.\n\n### Lecturer-specific spectrophotometer details (PDF pp. 372, 375–376, 380)\nThe lecturer describes a **spectrometer** that selects a wavelength and a **photometer** that measures the light intensity after it passes through a sample in a **cuvette**. In the described instrument, the photometer's electrical signal goes to a display, illustrated by a **galvanometer**. These are the lecture's instrument terms; contemporary instruments may use electronic digital detectors and displays.\n\nThe lecturer's example is **haemoglobin**: it appears red because it absorbs blue and green light more effectively than red light. Under appropriate conditions, the absorption at a selected wavelength can be used to estimate haemoglobin concentration. The slides also mention **water-quality photometers and colorimeters** used to estimate concentrations from colour intensity.\n\n**Understanding note:** The lecturer's descriptions concern colour-dependent absorption. Beer–Lambert calculations are useful additional context, but their equation is not established by the text on these pages and should not replace the lecturer's examinable instrument description.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Absorbance relates to transmitted light.\n- Beer–Lambert A = εlc under suitable conditions.\n- Use a blank and calibration.\n\n## Concept Connections\nThese concepts connect cell preparation, separation and biochemical interpretation: sample preparation affects which components survive, separation enriches material, and subsequent assays determine what is actually present.\n\n## Quick checks\n- Spectrophotometry can measure a sample’s **Answer:** Absorbance of light.\n- In Beer–Lambert law, l denotes **Answer:** Optical path length.\n- A blank is used mainly to **Answer:** Correct background from solvent and reagents.\n- Which equation defines absorbance? **Answer:** A = log10(I0/I).\n- Why might Beer–Lambert estimates fail? **Answer:** High concentration or scattering.","references":[],"attribution":"BIOL 151 lecture PDF pp. 371–381."},{"id":"BI02","title":"BI02 — Autoradiography and radioactive tracers","markdown":"# BI02 — Autoradiography and radioactive tracers\n\n## Core lesson\n\n### Principle\nAutoradiography detects the location of radioactive material using a radiation-sensitive detector. In classical film autoradiography, radioactive emissions sensitize **silver halide crystals** in the photographic emulsion. During photographic development, exposed crystals are converted to **metallic silver**; unsensitized crystals are removed during fixation. The resulting silver grains reveal the radioactive tracer distribution.\n\n### Using labelled precursors\nA radioactive precursor can be incorporated into molecules during a biological process. Its subsequent distribution provides evidence about where labelled material has accumulated, but not automatically the identity or activity of every labelled molecule.\n\n### Reading the image\nSilver grains or darkened regions correspond to detected emissions, with spatial resolution affected by isotope energy, specimen thickness and exposure. Appropriate controls and radiation-safety procedures are essential.\n\n### Autoradiography versus microscopy\nA conventional micrograph shows structural contrast; an autoradiograph maps a tracer signal. They can be correlated but answer different questions.\n\n### Lecturer-specific procedure and application \nIn classical autoradiography, the labelled specimen is placed in contact with **photographic emulsion**. Radiation sensitizes nearby **silver halide crystals**; photographic development produces **metallic silver grains** that indicate the distribution of radioactive material. The lecture lists **³H (tritium), ¹⁴C, ³²P, ⁴⁵Ca and ³⁵S** and gives **radioactively labelled amino acids** as an example for tracing sites of protein synthesis.\n\n**Scientific clarification:** The lecturer also mentions a **barium meal** in this sequence. A conventional barium meal uses radiopaque barium sulfate for X-ray contrast, **not a radioactive barium isotope** and **not autoradiography**. Preserve the example in lecture review notes, but do not teach the isotope claim as scientifically correct.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Autoradiography localizes radioactivity.\n- Silver halide crystals in the emulsion are converted to metallic silver during development; unexposed crystals are washed away during fixation.\n- Silver grains are detection products.\n- Signal localization is not proof of molecular identity.\n\n## Concept Connections\nThese concepts connect cell preparation, separation and biochemical interpretation: sample preparation affects which components survive, separation enriches material, and subsequent assays determine what is actually present.\n\n## Quick checks\n- Autoradiography primarily detects **Answer:** Location of radioactive tracer.\n- In film autoradiography, silver grains indicate **Answer:** Detected radiation exposure.\n- Radioactive precursor incorporation can indicate **Answer:** Distribution of labelled material.\n- Which affects autoradiographic spatial resolution? **Answer:** Isotope emission and specimen thickness.\n- An essential practice with autoradiography is **Answer:** Radiation safety and suitable controls.","references":["(PDF pp. 381–387)"],"attribution":"BIOL 151 lecture PDF pp. 382–387."},{"id":"BI03","title":"BI03 — Chromatography and partition principles","markdown":"# BI03 — Chromatography and partition principles\n\n## Core lesson\n\n### The two phases\nChromatography separates components through differences in their interactions with a stationary phase and a mobile phase. Components that spend relatively more time with the mobile phase generally migrate faster.\n\n### Partition chromatography\nIn partition-based separations, substances distribute differently between phases because of their relative solubility. Adsorption and other mechanisms may also contribute in real systems.\n\n### Changing separation\nSolvent composition, pH, temperature and stationary phase chemistry can alter retention and resolution. The correct conditions depend on the analytes, not a universal solvent recipe.\n\n### Interpreting movement\nIn a column example, the less strongly retained substance can elute first. Earlier elution alone does not identify a chemical; standards and controls are needed.\n\n### Lecturer-specific chromatography distinctions \nThe lecture defines chromatography as separation by distribution between a **stationary phase** and a **moving/mobile phase**. **Partition chromatography** depends mainly on the relative solubility of a substance in two immiscible liquid phases; a solute may dissolve unequally in them. The lecturer contrasts this with **adsorption chromatography**, where attraction to the stationary phase is emphasized, including electrostatic interactions, while noting that adsorption can also occur in partition chromatography.\n\n**Understanding note:** Partitioning and adsorption can coexist in real systems. The lecturer's contrast is a useful introductory distinction, not an absolute rule that all adsorption is purely electrostatic.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Stationary versus mobile phase.\n- Different affinities cause separation.\n- Elution order alone does not identify a compound.\n\n## Concept Connections\nThese concepts connect cell preparation, separation and biochemical interpretation: sample preparation affects which components survive, separation enriches material, and subsequent assays determine what is actually present.\n\n## Quick checks\n- The moving phase in chromatography is called the **Answer:** Mobile phase.\n- A compound strongly retained by stationary phase usually **Answer:** Moves more slowly.\n- Partition chromatography relies mainly on **Answer:** Different distribution between phases.\n- Which change can alter chromatographic retention? **Answer:** Mobile-phase composition.\n- Can early column elution alone identify a substance? **Answer:** No, standards and controls are needed.","references":["(PDF pp. 388–391)"],"attribution":"BIOL 151 lecture PDF pp. 388–391."},{"id":"BI04","title":"BI04 — Paper chromatography: setup and migration","markdown":"# BI04 — Paper chromatography: setup and migration\n\n## Core lesson\n\n### Preparing the paper\nApply a small concentrated sample spot on a pencil baseline, then place the paper in a developing chamber with the solvent level below the baseline. If the sample is submerged, it may wash directly into the solvent reservoir.\n\n### Development\nSolvent travels through paper by capillary action. Different sample components move different distances because their interactions with the mobile and stationary phases differ.\n\n### Recording a chromatogram\nRemove the paper before the solvent reaches the end, immediately mark the solvent front, dry it and locate the separated spots. Use suitable visualization for colourless compounds.\n\n### Interpretation limits\nA single solvent system may fail to resolve two substances. A spot at the same height as a standard suggests similarity under those conditions, not conclusive chemical identity.\n\n### Lecturer-specific practical details \nThe lecturer places a **small concentrated sample spot approximately 2 cm from the base** of the chromatography paper, often using a **capillary tube**. The developing solvent (examples: **ethanol, water or acetone**) must remain **below the spot**. Solvent travels through the paper by **capillary action**. Differences in solubility and attraction to the paper's cellulose affect migration. The lecture notes that development can take **minutes to hours**.\n\nIf two substances give the same Rf value in one solvent, the lecturer describes **two-way/two-dimensional chromatography**: turn the chromatogram **90°** and develop in a different solvent to improve separation. The lecture also notes that paper chromatography has largely been superseded in many applications by **thin-layer chromatography (TLC)**.\n\n**Understanding note:** Substances that bind irreversibly to the paper may not separate or be recovered appropriately using this method.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Baseline must stay above solvent.\n- Mark solvent front promptly.\n- Compare spots with standards cautiously.\n\n## Concept Connections\nThese concepts connect cell preparation, separation and biochemical interpretation: sample preparation affects which components survive, separation enriches material, and subsequent assays determine what is actually present.\n\n## Quick checks\n- In paper chromatography the sample baseline should be **Answer:** Above the solvent level.\n- Solvent rises through paper mainly by **Answer:** Capillary action.\n- When should the solvent front be marked? **Answer:** Immediately after removing the paper.\n- Why do mixture components travel different distances? **Answer:** Different interactions with two phases.\n- A spot matching a standard’s position proves **Answer:** Similarity under the tested conditions only.","references":["(PDF pp. 392–399)"],"attribution":"BIOL 151 lecture PDF pp. 392–401."},{"id":"BI05","title":"BI05 — Chromatograms and retention factor","markdown":"# BI05 — Chromatograms and retention factor\n\n## Core lesson\n\n### Reading a column chromatogram\nThe slide sequence shows solutes A and B moving down a column. B travels faster and emerges first; A is retained more strongly by the stationary phase. Changing solvent pH or temperature can change elution timing.\n\n### Retention factor in planar chromatography\nRf = distance travelled by the centre of a substance spot from the baseline ÷ distance travelled by the solvent front from the same baseline. Both distances must use the same units and the same starting line.\n\n### Worked example\nIf the spot moves 3.0 cm and the solvent front moves 6.0 cm, Rf = 3.0/6.0 = 0.50. For a valid conventional run, Rf is usually between 0 and 1.\n\n### What Rf can tell you\nRf depends on stationary phase, solvent, temperature and other conditions. Comparing Rf values is useful only under matching conditions, and a matching Rf alone does not prove two samples are identical.\n\n### Lecturer-specific chromatogram and Rf interpretation \nIn the illustrated **column chromatogram**, solvent carries solutes **A and B** down a column; **B moves faster and emerges first**, whereas A is more strongly retained. The lecture notes that changing **solvent pH or temperature** can change separation and elution.\n\nFor paper chromatography or TLC, **Rf = distance from origin to centre of the solute spot ÷ distance from origin to solvent front**. Both distances must be measured in the same direction and units. The lecturer calls Rf the **retention factor** on p. 398 and **“ratio of fronts”** on p. 404. Keep both lecture terms in the internal source record; use *retention factor* as the standard teaching term. Rf is useful for comparison **under identical experimental conditions**, not as a universally unique fingerprint.\n\n**Verified lecture worked TLC example :** The solvent front is **7.7 cm** from the origin. Spot 1 moves **1.4 cm**, so Rf = **1.4/7.7 = 0.18**; spot 2 moves **6.0 cm**, so Rf = **6.0/7.7 = 0.78**; spot 3 moves **4.1 cm**, so Rf = **4.1/7.7 = 0.53**. These are the values printed on the lecturer’s worked solution . Read the diagram and formula together: the numerator is always the distance to the **centre** of the substance spot from the same origin.\n\n## Slide Essentials\n- Rf = spot distance / solvent-front distance.\n- Column elution order reflects retention.\n- Rf comparison requires matched conditions.\n\n## Concept Connections\nThese concepts connect cell preparation, separation and biochemical interpretation: sample preparation affects which components survive, separation enriches material, and subsequent assays determine what is actually present.\n\n## Quick checks\n- Rf is calculated as **Answer:** Spot distance divided by solvent-front distance.\n- A spot moves 2 cm while solvent moves 5 cm. Its Rf is **Answer:** 0.40.\n- In the slide’s column example, solute B emerges first because it **Answer:** Is less strongly retained than A.\n- Rf values are most meaningfully compared when **Answer:** Experimental conditions match.\n- Two substances have the same Rf. What follows? **Answer:** Identity is not proven by Rf alone.","references":["(PDF pp. 402–410)","(PDF pp. 407–410)","(PDF p. 410)"],"attribution":"BIOL 151 lecture PDF pp. 402–410."}],"questions":[{"id":"CO-Q001","lesson":"CO01","question":"Which statement best defines a cell?","options":["Any structure that contains genetic material","A group of tissues that performs one function","The smallest visible structure in an organism","The smallest unit capable of performing life functions"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The lecture defines a cell as the smallest living unit capable of independently performing life functions."},{"id":"CO-Q002","lesson":"CO01","question":"Which is a general function of cells?","options":["Producing antibodies as a universal activity","Regulating the entry and exit of materials","Producing peptidoglycan in all organisms","Maintaining a constant shape in all tissues"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Regulating inflow and outflow is one of four general cellular functions on PDF p. 195. Antibody production is specialized, and peptidoglycan is not made by all cells."},{"id":"CO-Q003","lesson":"CO01","question":"What most directly determines the biochemical activities of a cell in the lecture?","options":["The total number of cells in the organism","Subcellular structure","The structure and organisation of its components","The distance between neighbouring cells"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The lecture states that biochemical activities are dictated by subcellular structure."},{"id":"CO-Q004","lesson":"CO01","question":"What do unicellular organisms demonstrate?","options":["Only multicellular life can reproduce","Cells never respond to stimuli","One cell can carry out major life functions","All cells require tissues to survive"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A single cell can exhibit major properties of life."},{"id":"CO-Q005","lesson":"CO01","question":"Which activity is NOT a general function of every cell?","options":["Directing cellular activity using genetic material","Compartmentalizing biochemical processes","Regulating exchange with the environment","Secreting antibodies to defend the organism"],"letter":"D","explanation":"PDF p. 195 lists compartmentalization, regulation of inflow and outflow, genetic direction of cell activities and continuity of life. Antibody secretion is restricted to specialized immune cells."},{"id":"CO-Q006","lesson":"CO01","question":"The cell is described as the basic structural and what other unit of life?","options":["Functional","Skeletal","Hormonal","Circulatory"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The cell is the basic structural and functional unit."},{"id":"CO-Q007","lesson":"CO02","question":"Which feature is most closely associated with differences in cell shape?","options":["A fixed cytoplasmic viscosity","Functional adaptation","All cells having equal volume","All cells having a cell wall"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The lecture explicitly lists functional adaptation as a determinant of shape."},{"id":"CO-Q008","lesson":"CO02","question":"Which factor is listed as influencing cell shape?","options":["The colour of the nucleus","The number of chromosomes alone","Mechanical action of adjoining cells","The number of organs in the body"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Mechanical forces exerted by neighbouring cells can affect shape."},{"id":"CO-Q009","lesson":"CO02","question":"The figure of 100 trillion cells in the human body should be understood as which of the following?","options":["A fixed value for every person","An estimate rather than an exact count","The number of cell types in the body","The number of cells in each organ"],"letter":"B","explanation":"PDF p. 197 states 100 trillion cells and about 200 cell types. Retain 100 trillion as the lecturer’s stated estimate, not a precise universal count; modern estimates differ."},{"id":"CO-Q010","lesson":"CO02","question":"Which statement best links cell form and function?","options":["Cell geometry can suit a specialized task","Every cell performs the same task","Shape never affects cell activity","Only the nucleus determines cell shape"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The lecture relates size and shape to function."},{"id":"CO-Q011","lesson":"CO02","question":"Which structure defines the outer boundary of a typical animal cell?","options":["Mitochondrial matrix","Plasma membrane","Nuclear envelope","Cell wall"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The plasma membrane bounds the cell."},{"id":"CO-Q012","lesson":"CO02","question":"Which factor is explicitly identified as influencing cell shape?","options":["The number of mitochondria alone","The quantity of DNA per chromosome","The mechanical action of adjoining cells","The color of the plasma membrane"],"letter":"C","explanation":"PDF p. 200 lists surface tension and viscosity of cytoplasm, mechanical action of adjoining cells, membrane rigidity and functional adaptation. Mechanical influence from neighbouring cells is a listed factor. The membrane cortex/cytoskeleton also contributes to mechanical properties."},{"id":"CO-Q013","lesson":"CO03","question":"What is the total surface area of a cube with side 2 cm?","options":["8 cm²","12 cm²","16 cm²","24 cm²"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Six faces, each of area 4 cm², give 24 cm²."},{"id":"CO-Q014","lesson":"CO03","question":"What is the volume of a cube with side 4 cm?","options":["64 cm³","16 cm³","96 cm³","24 cm³"],"letter":"A","explanation":"V = L³ = 4³ = 64 cm³."},{"id":"CO-Q015","lesson":"CO03","question":"What is the SA:V ratio for a cube with side 4 cm?","options":["4 cm⁻¹","1.5 cm⁻¹","6 cm⁻¹","3 cm⁻¹"],"letter":"B","explanation":"SA:V = 96/64 = 1.5 cm⁻¹."},{"id":"CO-Q016","lesson":"CO03","question":"If the side length of a cube doubles, by what factor does its volume increase?","options":["4","16","8","2"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A cube has volume L³. Doubling L gives (2L)³ = 8L³. The lecture’s surface-area-to-volume table on PDF page 202 demonstrates this relationship."},{"id":"CO-Q017","lesson":"CO03","question":"As a cube increases in side length, what happens to its surface-area-to-volume ratio?","options":["Volume increases more slowly than surface area","The SA:V ratio stays constant","The exchange surface becomes zero","Surface area per unit volume decreases"],"letter":"D","explanation":"For a cube, surface area/volume = 6/L. Thus the ratio decreases as side length increases, consistent with the lecture table on PDF page 202."},{"id":"CO-Q018","lesson":"CO03","question":"Why does SA:V help explain microscopic cell size?","options":["Exchange surface does not keep pace with internal volume","Cells contain no transport proteins","Large cells cannot carry out reactions","Cell membranes have no permeability"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Volume-related demands can rise faster than exchange area."},{"id":"CO-Q019","lesson":"CO04","question":"What is the defining feature of a typical eukaryotic cell?","options":["DNA in a nucleoid only","A membrane-bound nucleus","A peptidoglycan wall","Absence of ribosomes"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Eukaryotes characteristically have a membrane-bound nucleus."},{"id":"CO-Q020","lesson":"CO04","question":"Which component is characteristic of most bacterial cell walls?","options":["Chitin","Collagen","Peptidoglycan","Cellulose"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The lecture specifies peptidoglycan for bacteria."},{"id":"CO-Q021","lesson":"CO04","question":"Which structure is present in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes?","options":["Mitochondria","Golgi apparatus","Nuclear envelope","Ribosomes"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Both use ribosomes for protein synthesis."},{"id":"CO-Q022","lesson":"CO04","question":"Which statement about archaeal cell walls is most accurate?","options":["They lack bacterial peptidoglycan","They always contain bacterial peptidoglycan","They are identical to plant cellulose walls","All archaea lack any outer boundary"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Archaeal walls use chemically distinct materials where walls exist."},{"id":"CO-Q023","lesson":"CO04","question":"Which statement accurately qualifies the lecture’s generalization that prokaryotic DNA is not wrapped around histones?","options":["DNA packaging never varies across domains","Some archaea possess histone proteins","All bacteria have nucleosomes identical to human cells","All prokaryotes possess a membrane-bound nucleus"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The slide describes prokaryotes generally; some archaea do have histone proteins, whereas bacteria usually use other DNA-associated proteins. This is a scientific clarification, not a replacement of the examinable slide wording."},{"id":"CO-Q024","lesson":"CO04","question":"Which statement is part of cell theory?","options":["Cells arise spontaneously from nonliving material","All living organisms are composed of one or more cells","Only animal cells contain genetic information","All cells have a membrane-bound nucleus"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Cell theory holds that organisms consist of cells, the cell is a basic unit of life and cells arise from pre-existing cells. PDF p. 201 assigns cell theory for study but does not explain it; this is explicitly supplementary learning content."},{"id":"OR01-Q01","lesson":"OR01","question":"Which membrane arrangement is characteristic of the microbodies described in the lecture?","options":["A single limiting membrane","Two membranes with cristae","No membrane at all","A cell wall surrounding them"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The lecture defines microbodies as small single-membrane organelles."},{"id":"OR01-Q02","lesson":"OR01","question":"Which peroxisomal enzyme decomposes hydrogen peroxide?","options":["Urate oxidase","Catalase","Hexosaminidase A","RNA polymerase"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Catalase converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen."},{"id":"OR01-Q03","lesson":"OR01","question":"Which statement about human urate oxidase is most accurate?","options":["It is a principal human liver enzyme","It makes ATP in human peroxisomes","Humans lack functional urate oxidase","It is secreted by human lysosomes"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The lecture lists urate oxidase generally, but humans do not possess a functional uricase."},{"id":"OR01-Q04","lesson":"OR01","question":"Which process is associated with peroxisomes?","options":["Ribosome assembly","Chromosome segregation","DNA transcription","Fatty-acid oxidation"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Peroxisomes participate in oxidative metabolism, including fatty-acid breakdown."},{"id":"OR01-Q05","lesson":"OR01","question":"Why must cells break down the hydrogen peroxide generated during peroxisomal oxidation?","options":["It can damage cellular components by oxidation","It is the main source of chromatin","It directly builds nuclear pores","It prevents all enzyme reactions"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Peroxisomal oxidases can produce hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂), which can damage cellular molecules. Catalase decomposes H₂O₂. Note: the lecturer lists urate oxidase among peroxisomal enzymes; functional urate oxidase is absent in humans."},{"id":"OR02-Q01","lesson":"OR02","question":"What is the central metabolic importance of glyoxysomes in oil-rich seeds?","options":["Formation of the mitotic spindle","Conversion of stored fat carbon toward sugars","Assembly of ribosomal subunits","Digestion of inhaled silica"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The glyoxylate cycle supports conversion of lipid-derived acetyl-CoA into intermediates used to form carbohydrates."},{"id":"OR02-Q02","lesson":"OR02","question":"What is the net product specifically named in the slide for the glyoxylate cycle?","options":["Lactate","Glucose","Succinate","Urate"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The lecture identifies succinate as a net glyoxylate-cycle product."},{"id":"OR02-Q03","lesson":"OR02","question":"Which by-product gives hydrogenosomes their name?","options":["Oxygen","Ammonia","Carbon dioxide","Molecular hydrogen"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Hydrogenosomes release H₂ during certain anaerobic energy-generating pathways."},{"id":"OR02-Q04","lesson":"OR02","question":"In which environment are hydrogenosomes particularly associated?","options":["Anaerobic eukaryotic cells","Mature human erythrocytes","Chloroplasts of green leaves","Extracellular collagen fibres"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Hydrogenosomes occur in some oxygen-independent eukaryotes."},{"id":"OR02-Q05","lesson":"OR02","question":"Which term is listed in the microbody family but not explained in detail by these slides?","options":["Centrosome","Glycosome","Nucleolus","Phagosome"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Glycosomes are named in the source list but not developed in this section."},{"id":"OR03-Q01","lesson":"OR03","question":"What maintains the acidic lumen of a lysosome?","options":["A DNA helicase","A ribosomal subunit","An H⁺-ATPase","A centriole triplet"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The proton pump uses ATP to move H⁺ into lysosomes."},{"id":"OR03-Q02","lesson":"OR03","question":"The lecturer states that H⁺ pumping acidifies the lysosome lumen to approximately what pH?","options":["7.4","6.8","5.0","2.0"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Lysosomal proton pumps maintain an acidic lumen around pH 5.0, as stated on the slide. This is an approximate value, not a universal fixed pH."},{"id":"OR03-Q03","lesson":"OR03","question":"Which process digests components originating inside the cell?","options":["Autophagy","Heterophagy","Transcription","Glycolysis"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Autophagy recycles the cell’s own structures."},{"id":"OR03-Q04","lesson":"OR03","question":"Which vesicle initially contains material engulfed by phagocytosis?","options":["Nucleosome","Phagosome","Peroxisome","Centrosome"],"letter":"B","explanation":"A phagosome forms around an engulfed particle and can fuse with lysosomal compartments."},{"id":"OR03-Q05","lesson":"OR03","question":"What is a residual body?","options":["A site of rRNA transcription","A membrane-less DNA compartment","A compartment with undigested remains","A precursor of a centriole"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Residual bodies retain material not fully degraded by lysosomal enzymes."},{"id":"OR04-Q01","lesson":"OR04","question":"Which consequence of persistent silica-induced lung injury is emphasised in the lecture?","options":["Higher nuclear pore permeability","Loss of ribosomal subunits","Increased glyoxylate-cycle activity","Collagen deposition and fibrosis"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Phagocyte injury and inflammation can promote fibroblast-driven fibrosis."},{"id":"OR04-Q02","lesson":"OR04","question":"What is the deficient enzyme in Tay–Sachs disease?","options":["β-Hexosaminidase A","Catalase","Urate oxidase","DNA polymerase"],"letter":"A","explanation":"HEXA-associated deficiency of β-hexosaminidase A leads to GM2 accumulation."},{"id":"OR04-Q03","lesson":"OR04","question":"What accumulates in neurons in Tay–Sachs disease?","options":["Succinate","GM2 ganglioside","Hydrogen peroxide","Collagen"],"letter":"B","explanation":"GM2 is the lysosomal substrate that cannot be adequately degraded."},{"id":"OR04-Q04","lesson":"OR04","question":"Which statement about Tay–Sachs ancestry is accurate?","options":["It occurs only in Eastern Europe","It cannot affect African populations","It can occur in any ancestry","It is caused by nationality"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Some populations have higher carrier frequencies, but the disease is not exclusive to them."},{"id":"OR04-Q05","lesson":"OR04","question":"What does lysosomal enzyme deficiency typically cause?","options":["Immediate chromosome duplication","An increase in nuclear pore number","Centriole disappearance","Accumulation of undegraded substrate"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Substrate storage is the defining consequence of many lysosomal storage diseases."},{"id":"OR05-Q01","lesson":"OR05","question":"What is continuous with the outer nuclear membrane?","options":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Lysosomal lumen","Centriolar microtubules","The plasma membrane"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The outer nuclear envelope is continuous with rough ER."},{"id":"OR05-Q02","lesson":"OR05","question":"What regulates selective exchange between nucleus and cytoplasm?","options":["Catalase","Nuclear pore complexes","Glyoxysomes","Collagen fibres"],"letter":"B","explanation":"NPCs control transport across the nuclear envelope."},{"id":"OR05-Q03","lesson":"OR05","question":"How many chromosomes are normally present in a typical human diploid somatic cell?","options":["23","92","46","44"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A typical human diploid somatic cell has 46 chromosomes; gametes have 23."},{"id":"OR05-Q04","lesson":"OR05","question":"What is the basic repeating DNA-packaging unit of chromatin?","options":["Hydrogenosome","Phagosome","Peroxisome","Nucleosome"],"letter":"D","explanation":"DNA wrapped around histone proteins forms a nucleosome."},{"id":"OR05-Q05","lesson":"OR05","question":"What does transcription produce from a DNA template?","options":["RNA","Newly assembled collagen fibres","Centrioles","Hydrogen peroxide"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Transcription synthesises RNA using DNA information."},{"id":"OR06-Q01","lesson":"OR06","question":"Which process occurs prominently in the nucleolus?","options":["Fatty-acid oxidation","rRNA production and ribosomal subunit assembly","Digestion of silica","Formation of peroxisomal catalase"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The nucleolus synthesises rRNA and assembles ribosomal subunits."},{"id":"OR06-Q02","lesson":"OR06","question":"What is the membrane status of the nucleolus?","options":["Double membrane","Single membrane","No surrounding membrane","A bacterial cell wall"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The nucleolus is a membrane-less nuclear region."},{"id":"OR06-Q03","lesson":"OR06","question":"Which chromosomal region is associated with nucleolus formation?","options":["Centromere only","Telomere only","Lysosomal membrane","Nucleolar organiser region"],"letter":"D","explanation":"NORs contain rRNA genes and help nucleoli form."},{"id":"OR06-Q04","lesson":"OR06","question":"Which statement best describes chromatin?","options":["DNA with associated proteins","Only free RNA","Only condensed chromosomes in mitosis","A network of microtubules"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Chromatin comprises DNA and proteins including histones."},{"id":"OR06-Q05","lesson":"OR06","question":"Which statement about nucleolar number is scientifically best?","options":["Every species has exactly four nucleoli","It can vary with cell type and activity","Every cell contains one nucleolus","Nucleoli persist unchanged through mitosis"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The slide gives a fixed-number generalisation, but nucleolar number varies."},{"id":"OR07-Q01","lesson":"OR07","question":"How many microtubule triplets occur around a typical centriole?","options":["Seven","Eight","Nine","Ten"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A centriole has nine peripheral triplets (9 × 3)."},{"id":"OR07-Q02","lesson":"OR07","question":"Approximately what angle do the two centrioles in a typical centrosome form?","options":["0°","30°","45°","Approximately 90°"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The slide on PDF page 241 describes the two centrioles as oriented at approximately 90 degrees to each other."},{"id":"OR07-Q03","lesson":"OR07","question":"What is a major role of centrosomes during animal-cell division?","options":["Organisation of the mitotic spindle","Synthesis of lysosomal hydrolases","Degradation of GM2 ganglioside","Transcription of rRNA"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Centrosomes organise spindle microtubules."},{"id":"OR07-Q04","lesson":"OR07","question":"Which end of a typical radial centrosomal microtubule extends toward the cell edge?","options":["Minus end","Plus end","Both ends equally anchored","Neither end"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Plus ends usually face outward and are more dynamic."},{"id":"OR07-Q05","lesson":"OR07","question":"What can a centriole become to organise a cilium?","options":["A nucleosome","A residual body","A basal body","A glyoxysome"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Basal bodies derive from centrioles and organise ciliary microtubules."},{"id":"CYQ01","lesson":"CY01","question":"Which filament has an outer diameter of approximately 25 nm?","options":["Actin filament","Microtubule","Intermediate filament","Keratin filament"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The lecture lists microtubules at about 25 nm."},{"id":"CYQ02","lesson":"CY01","question":"Which protein forms the main subunits of microfilaments?","options":["Actin","Tubulin","Keratin","Dynein"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Microfilaments are actin filaments."},{"id":"CYQ03","lesson":"CY01","question":"Which motor protein typically operates on actin filaments?","options":["Kinesin","Dynein","Myosin","Tau"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Myosin motors interact with actin."},{"id":"CYQ04","lesson":"CY01","question":"What mechanical property is most closely associated with intermediate filaments?","options":["Resistance to stretching and mechanical stress","Rapid shortening driven by myosin motors","ATP-dependent transport of vesicles","Formation of a hollow tubulin-based track"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Intermediate filaments provide tensile strength and protect cells from mechanical deformation. Myosin acts with actin; vesicle transport often uses microtubules."},{"id":"CYQ05","lesson":"CY01","question":"Which three protein families are identified as cytoskeletal motors?","options":["Kinesin, dynein, myosin","Actin, tubulin, keratin","Tau, actin, collagen","Kinesin, keratin, laminin"],"letter":"A","explanation":"These are the three motor families listed in the slides."},{"id":"CYQ06","lesson":"CY01","question":"Which set of filament diameters matches the lecture slide?","options":["Microtubules ~10 nm; intermediate filaments ~25 nm; actin ~8 nm","Microtubules ~25 nm; intermediate filaments ~10 nm; actin ~8 nm","Microtubules ~8 nm; intermediate filaments ~10 nm; actin ~25 nm","Microtubules ~25 nm; intermediate filaments ~8 nm; actin ~10 nm"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The lecturer lists external diameters: microtubules ~25 nm, intermediate filaments ~10 nm, and actin filaments ~8 nm."},{"id":"CYQ07","lesson":"CY02","question":"Which cellular structure helps red blood cells maintain a flexible biconcave shape?","options":["Nucleolus","Membrane-associated cytoskeleton","Mitotic spindle","Golgi stack"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The membrane skeleton contributes to red-cell shape and deformability."},{"id":"CYQ08","lesson":"CY02","question":"What is a protofilament in a microtubule?","options":["A membrane pore","An ATPase complex","A chromosome arm","A linear chain of tubulin dimers"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Protofilaments are longitudinal tubulin polymers."},{"id":"CYQ09","lesson":"CY02","question":"How many protofilaments usually make up a cytoplasmic microtubule wall?","options":["13","8","10","25"],"letter":"A","explanation":"A typical microtubule contains 13 protofilaments."},{"id":"CYQ10","lesson":"CY02","question":"Which pair forms the tubulin dimer?","options":["Actin and myosin","Kinesin and dynein","Alpha- and beta-tubulin","Keratin and tau"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Microtubule subunits are α/β-tubulin heterodimers."},{"id":"CYQ11","lesson":"CY02","question":"The plus and minus ends of a microtubule refer to what?","options":["Opposite electrical charges","Structural polarity","Different organelles","Two cell types"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Plus/minus describe structural orientation, not electric charge."},{"id":"CYQ12","lesson":"CY02","question":"Which process is called dynamic instability?","options":["Permanent filament rigidity","Motor-protein synthesis","Chromosome duplication","Switching between growth and shrinkage"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Microtubule ends alternate between assembly and rapid shortening."},{"id":"CYQ13","lesson":"CY03","question":"What is a major microtubule-organizing centre in animal cells?","options":["Centrosome","Lysosome","Peroxisome","Nucleolus"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The centrosome is a principal MTOC in many animal cells."},{"id":"CYQ14","lesson":"CY03","question":"Which microtubule-associated protein is implicated in Alzheimer’s disease?","options":["Myosin","Keratin","Tau","Actin"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Tau binds microtubules and its abnormal phosphorylation/aggregation is implicated in Alzheimer’s disease. The lecture also describes neurofilament changes; do not equate Alzheimer’s disease solely with neurofilament defects."},{"id":"CYQ15","lesson":"CY03","question":"What does microtubule polarity enable?","options":["Production of ATP","Directional organization of tracks","Protein translation","DNA transcription"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Directional asymmetry supports regulated organization and motor transport."},{"id":"CYQ16","lesson":"CY03","question":"A patient has skin blistering caused by defective keratin filament networks. Which condition is most consistent?","options":["Tay–Sachs disease","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Huntington disease"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The slide on PDF p. 271 associates epidermolysis bullosa simplex with defective keratin bundles and skin blistering. Keratin 5/14 variants are common molecular causes."},{"id":"CYQ17","lesson":"CY03","question":"What is the main cytoskeletal role of a mitotic spindle?","options":["Chromosome alignment and segregation","Lipid synthesis","Protein glycosylation","Waste digestion"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Spindle microtubules position and separate chromosomes."},{"id":"CYQ18","lesson":"CY03","question":"What does paclitaxel (Taxol) do to microtubules?","options":["Destroys tubulin genes","Prevents ATP synthesis","Stabilizes them against disassembly","Converts them into actin"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Paclitaxel suppresses normal microtubule disassembly and dynamics."},{"id":"CYQ19","lesson":"CY04","question":"Which drug is named in the slides as used in cancer chemotherapy?","options":["Paclitaxel","Vincristine","Tubulin","Kinesin"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Vincristine is an established anticancer vinca alkaloid."},{"id":"CYQ20","lesson":"CY04","question":"Which change would also disrupt mitosis despite preserving polymerized microtubules?","options":["Increasing DNA repair","Increasing ribosome assembly","Raising cell glucose","Preventing microtubule disassembly"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Over-stabilized microtubules cannot remodel normally during mitosis."},{"id":"CYQ21","lesson":"CY04","question":"Which cytoskeletal class is about 10 nm in diameter?","options":["Intermediate filaments","Actin filaments","Microtubules","Myosin motors"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The slide gives intermediate filaments as approximately 10 nm."},{"id":"CYQ22","lesson":"CY04","question":"Which motor families commonly travel on microtubules?","options":["Myosin and actin","Keratin and laminin","Kinesin and dynein","Tau and collagen"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Kinesins and dyneins are microtubule-based motors."},{"id":"CYQ23","lesson":"CY04","question":"Which statement best describes the cytoskeleton?","options":["An unchanging cell wall","A dynamic filament network","A DNA-storage compartment","A single membrane layer"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The cytoskeleton continually reorganizes."},{"id":"CYQ24","lesson":"CY04","question":"Why can microtubule-targeting drugs affect healthy cells?","options":["Only healthy cells contain tubulin","Normal cells never divide","They directly remove all DNA","Normal cells also require microtubules"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Normal cells rely on microtubules for division and intracellular transport."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-84","lesson":"CM01","question":"Which term refers to the aqueous phase of cytoplasm outside membrane-enclosed organelles?","options":["Cytosol","Nucleoplasm","Chromatin","Glycocalyx"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Cytosol is the aqueous component of the cytoplasm; cytoplasm includes organelles."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-85","lesson":"CM01","question":"Which component of a phospholipid is most directly hydrophobic?","options":["Phosphate head","Fatty-acid tails","Polar head group","Attached carbohydrate"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The nonpolar hydrocarbon tails avoid water."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-86","lesson":"CM01","question":"Why do phospholipids form a bilayer in water?","options":["Their tails bond to water","Their heads repel all ions","Their amphipathic regions orient appropriately","Their proteins assemble first"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Hydrophilic heads face water while hydrophobic tails cluster inward."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-87","lesson":"CM01","question":"What is the central idea of the fluid mosaic model?","options":["A rigid protein shell","A mobile lipid bilayer with associated proteins","A single lipid monolayer","A membrane made entirely of proteins"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Membrane proteins are embedded in or associated with a fluid lipid matrix."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-88","lesson":"CM01","question":"Which inclusion is explicitly listed in the cytosol slides?","options":["Glycogen granule","Nuclear pore","Crista","Centriole"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The slides identify glycogen granules and lipid droplets as cytosolic inclusions."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-89","lesson":"CM01","question":"Which structure separates the cell interior from the external environment?","options":["Nucleolus","Plasma membrane","Centrosome","Ribosome"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The plasma membrane forms the cell boundary."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-90","lesson":"CM02","question":"What is lateral diffusion in a membrane?","options":["Movement between the two leaflets","Movement along the plane of one leaflet","Removal of cholesterol","Transport through a channel"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Lateral movement is within the bilayer plane, unlike flip-flop."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-91","lesson":"CM02","question":"Which lipid tails usually reduce tight packing?","options":["Fully saturated tails","Cis-unsaturated tails","Identical straight tails","Longer straight tails"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Cis double bonds introduce bends that hinder close packing."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-92","lesson":"CM02","question":"At relatively low temperatures, how does cholesterol generally affect phospholipid packing?","options":["Promotes tighter packing","Eliminates the bilayer","Reduces close packing","Converts lipids to proteins"],"letter":"C","explanation":"At low temperatures, cholesterol disrupts tight phospholipid packing and can reduce membrane solidification. At higher temperatures it restrains excessive phospholipid movement; the effect is temperature-dependent."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-93","lesson":"CM02","question":"Which movement is relatively rare without specialized enzymes?","options":["Lateral diffusion","Protein rotation","Transverse flip-flop","Local tail flexing"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Spontaneous transfer between leaflets is energetically unfavorable."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-94","lesson":"CM02","question":"As temperature decreases, membrane lipid movement generally does what?","options":["Increases indefinitely","Decreases","Becomes independent of composition","Converts to active transport"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Cooling reduces molecular motion and may cause a phase transition."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-95","lesson":"CM02","question":"At relatively high temperatures, cholesterol in animal-cell membranes generally","options":["Increases spontaneous lipid flip-flop markedly","Restrains excessive movement of phospholipid tails","Converts the lipid bilayer into a monolayer","Prevents all diffusion of membrane proteins"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Cholesterol tends to restrain excessive phospholipid mobility at higher temperatures, while disrupting close packing at lower temperatures. It does not stop diffusion entirely."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-96","lesson":"CM03","question":"Which membrane protein spans the entire lipid bilayer?","options":["Peripheral protein","Transmembrane protein","Soluble cytosolic protein","Nucleolar protein"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Transmembrane proteins cross the bilayer."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-97","lesson":"CM03","question":"Which cell-surface structure is rich in carbohydrate groups?","options":["Glycocalyx","Microtubule","Nucleolus","Centrosome"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The glycocalyx includes carbohydrate chains from glycoproteins and glycolipids."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-98","lesson":"CM03","question":"What is a key distinction of peripheral membrane proteins?","options":["They always span the bilayer","They bind relatively loosely to membrane surfaces","They are all ion channels","They contain only lipids"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Peripheral proteins associate with surfaces or other proteins rather than spanning the core."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-99","lesson":"CM03","question":"Which protein type binds a signal and initiates a cellular response?","options":["Receptor","Structural phospholipid","Glycogen granule","Cholesterol"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Receptors detect extracellular or intracellular signals."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-100","lesson":"CM03","question":"Which protein type typically binds a solute and changes conformation to move it?","options":["Transporter","A protein that only anchors the membrane to the cytoskeleton","A ligand-binding receptor that triggers a signalling cascade","An enzyme that catalyses a reaction without moving a solute"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Carrier transporters bind substrates and change shape during transport."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-101","lesson":"CM03","question":"Where are plasma-membrane glycolipid carbohydrate groups generally oriented?","options":["Toward extracellular space","Only toward cytosol","Inside the hydrophobic core","Toward the nucleus"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The lecture depicts glycolipid sugars on the extracellular side."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-102","lesson":"CM04","question":"Which molecule generally crosses the lipid bilayer relatively readily?","options":["A hydrated sodium ion","Oxygen","A large folded protein","A DNA fragment"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Small nonpolar gases diffuse through the lipid core."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-103","lesson":"CM04","question":"What is the combined effect of concentration and voltage on ion movement called?","options":["Osmotic pressure only","Electrochemical gradient","Hydrostatic gradient","Protein folding"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Ion movement depends on chemical and electrical driving forces."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-104","lesson":"CM04","question":"Which protein can greatly facilitate water movement across many cell membranes?","options":["Aquaporin","Histone","Tubulin","DNA polymerase"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Aquaporins are water channels; water can also cross lipid bilayers slowly."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-105","lesson":"CM04","question":"Which process can move large cargo across a cell membrane?","options":["Simple diffusion through lipid tails","Vesicular transport","Lateral lipid diffusion","Spontaneous flip-flop"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Endocytosis and exocytosis move material using vesicles."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-106","lesson":"CM04","question":"In a typical animal cell, which ion is more concentrated inside than outside?","options":["Sodium","Potassium","Free calcium in the cytosol","Chloride in every animal-cell type"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The slides give higher intracellular K+ and higher extracellular Na+."},{"id":"CM_MCQ_24-107","lesson":"CM04","question":"What is a membrane potential?","options":["A difference in voltage across the membrane","The total number of phospholipids","A difference in temperature","The concentration of cholesterol"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Charge separation creates a voltage difference across the membrane."},{"id":"CE01-Q01","lesson":"CE01","question":"Which stage follows homogenisation in cell fractionation?","options":["Protein translation","Separation of cell components","DNA replication","Fixation for microscopy"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Homogenisation makes a mixture; separation isolates fractions."},{"id":"CE01-Q02","lesson":"CE01","question":"Why use an iso-osmotic medium?","options":["To protect organelles against osmotic damage","To stain nuclei","To dissolve all membranes","To increase radioactive decay"],"letter":"A","explanation":"An appropriate osmotic environment helps preserve organelle structure."},{"id":"CE01-Q03","lesson":"CE01","question":"Which technique uses high-frequency sound?","options":["Sedimentation analysis","Autoradiography","Chromatography","Sonication"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Sonication disrupts cells with sound-induced mechanical effects."},{"id":"CE01-Q04","lesson":"CE01","question":"Which method is especially useful for tough plant tissue?","options":["Beer–Lambert assay","Rate-zonal separation","Autoradiography","Grinding with mortar and pestle"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Mechanical grinding breaks relatively rigid plant tissues."},{"id":"CE01-Q05","lesson":"CE01","question":"What is a homogenate?","options":["Suspension after cell disruption","A purified enzyme crystal","A chromatographic solvent","A photographic emulsion"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The disrupted cellular mixture is called the homogenate."},{"id":"CE02-Q01","lesson":"CE02","question":"The liquid remaining above a centrifuged pellet is the","options":["Supernatant","Stationary phase","Solvent front","Homogenate pellet"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Supernatant is the liquid above sedimented material."},{"id":"CE02-Q02","lesson":"CE02","question":"What sequence best describes differential centrifugation of a cell homogenate?","options":["One equilibrium-density gradient followed by no additional spins","A series of spins at progressively higher centrifugal forces","One spin that produces completely pure organelle fractions","A series of spins at progressively lower centrifugal forces"],"letter":"B","explanation":"In differential centrifugation, the supernatant is spun successively at higher forces to sediment progressively smaller/lighter components; fractions are enriched, not automatically pure."},{"id":"CE02-Q03","lesson":"CE02","question":"A low-speed pellet is most likely enriched in","options":["Only free amino acids","Pure ribosomes","Nuclei and large debris","Only soluble salts"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Large particles tend to sediment first."},{"id":"CE02-Q04","lesson":"CE02","question":"Why is rpm alone insufficient to specify centrifugal force?","options":["Rotor radius also affects RCF","Only tube colour matters","All rotors have identical radius","RPM always equals ×g"],"letter":"A","explanation":"RCF depends on both rotational speed and rotor radius."},{"id":"CE02-Q05","lesson":"CE02","question":"An organelle fraction from differential centrifugation is generally","options":["Identical to the starting homogenate","Always free of membrane fragments","Guaranteed chemically pure","Enriched rather than completely pure"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Different structures can co-sediment."},{"id":"CE03-Q01","lesson":"CE03","question":"Two proteins have similar buoyant densities but different sedimentation speeds. Which technique separates them chiefly by their rate of movement through a gradient?","options":["Isopycnic centrifugation","Rate-zonal centrifugation","Equilibrium CsCl banding","Simple dialysis"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Rate-zonal centrifugation separates by sedimentation rate, influenced by size, mass and shape. Isopycnic separation instead resolves equilibrium buoyant density."},{"id":"CE03-Q02","lesson":"CE03","question":"During isopycnic centrifugation, a particle reaches its equilibrium band when","options":["Its diameter equals the tube diameter","Its sedimentation coefficient becomes zero in vacuum","Its buoyant density matches the surrounding gradient","Its molecular mass matches the solvent mass"],"letter":"C","explanation":"At equilibrium, the particle bands where its buoyant density equals that of the gradient medium; size alone does not define the equilibrium position."},{"id":"CE03-Q03","lesson":"CE03","question":"Why must a rate-zonal gradient centrifugation run be stopped before all components pellet?","options":["To keep separated zones at different positions","To ensure particles reach equal density","To remove the gradient solvent completely","To convert it into an isopycnic run"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Rate-zonal separation depends on different sedimentation distances; if particles pellet, the resolved zones are lost."},{"id":"CE03-Q04","lesson":"CE03","question":"Which experiment is best suited to separating nucleic acids according to equilibrium buoyant density?","options":["A rate-zonal run terminated early","Differential centrifugation of nuclei","A sucrose wash with no centrifugal field","Isopycnic centrifugation in CsCl"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The lecturer identifies CsCl isopycnic gradients for nucleic acids; bands form at matching buoyant density."},{"id":"CE03-Q05","lesson":"CE03","question":"After collecting organelle bands from a density gradient, what best establishes which organelles are in each fraction?","options":["Position in the tube without other measurements","Organelle-specific enzyme markers or other assays","The colour of the rotor used for the run","The fact that each band is automatically pure"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Gradient position alone cannot prove identity or purity. Marker enzymes, microscopy or appropriate assays are needed to characterize fractions."},{"id":"CE04-Q01","lesson":"CE04","question":"The primary goal of preparative centrifugation is to","options":["Find Rf values","Recover material for further study","Measure visible-light wavelength","Measure film exposure"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Preparative methods collect fractions."},{"id":"CE04-Q02","lesson":"CE04","question":"Analytical ultracentrifugation studies","options":["Microscope eyepiece power","Solvent fronts on paper","Radioactive silver grains","Sedimentation behavior in solution"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Analytical methods quantify macromolecular behavior."},{"id":"CE04-Q03","lesson":"CE04","question":"Which statement correctly describes the Svedberg (S) unit?","options":["It expresses sedimentation coefficient, with 1 S = 10⁻¹³ s","It measures particle diameter, with 1 S = 10⁻¹³ m","It measures buoyant density, with 1 S = 10⁻¹³ g/mL","It expresses angular speed, with 1 S = 10⁻¹³ rpm"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The sedimentation coefficient has units of time; one svedberg equals 10⁻¹³ seconds. It depends on mass, shape, friction and buoyancy."},{"id":"CE04-Q04","lesson":"CE04","question":"Why is sedimentation coefficient not mass alone?","options":["It cannot vary with solvent","Shape and friction also affect it","It depends only on sample colour","It is always equal to molecular weight"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Sedimentation also depends on buoyancy and hydrodynamics."},{"id":"CE04-Q05","lesson":"CE04","question":"Which parameter is essential for safe centrifuge use?","options":["Film grain size","Rotor-rated speed and tube compatibility","Visible-light hue","Spot colour on paper"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Rotor and tube limits must be respected."},{"id":"BI01-Q01","lesson":"BI01","question":"Spectrophotometry can measure a sample’s","options":["Centrifuge rotor radius only","Rate of radioactive decay only","Absorbance of light","Spot movement without solvent"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Spectrophotometry quantifies light absorption or transmission."},{"id":"BI01-Q02","lesson":"BI01","question":"In the Beer–Lambert relationship A = εlc, what does l represent?","options":["Spot diameter","Radioactive half-life","Sedimentation rate","Optical path length"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The variable l is optical path length, commonly in cm. ε is molar absorptivity and c is concentration. The equation assumes suitable measurement conditions."},{"id":"BI01-Q03","lesson":"BI01","question":"A blank is used mainly to","options":["Generate radioactivity","Correct background from solvent and reagents","Identify every unknown molecule","Increase sample concentration"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Blanking accounts for baseline absorbance."},{"id":"BI01-Q04","lesson":"BI01","question":"Which equation defines absorbance?","options":["A = RCF / rpm","A = I0 × I","A = distance spot / time","A = log10(I0/I)"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Absorbance is the log of incident over transmitted intensity."},{"id":"BI01-Q05","lesson":"BI01","question":"Why might Beer–Lambert estimates fail?","options":["Correct blank selection","High concentration or scattering","A valid linear calibration","Stable wavelength"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Nonlinear conditions can invalidate direct concentration estimates."},{"id":"BI02-Q01","lesson":"BI02","question":"Autoradiography primarily detects","options":["Cellular pH with no probe","Membrane fluidity directly","Protein mass by centrifuge only","Location of radioactive tracer"],"letter":"D","explanation":"It maps emissions from labelled material."},{"id":"BI02-Q02","lesson":"BI02","question":"What happens to the exposed silver halide crystals during conventional film autoradiography?","options":["They become radioactive isotopes","They are converted into metallic silver during development","They dissolve into the specimen before exposure","They are converted directly into barium sulfate"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Radiation produces a latent image in silver-halide emulsion; photographic development yields metallic silver at exposed sites. Unexposed silver halide is removed during photographic fixing."},{"id":"BI02-Q03","lesson":"BI02","question":"After incubating cells with a radioactive precursor, what can autoradiography most directly show?","options":["Exact molecular identity of every labelled compound","The absolute number of enzymes in each organelle","Spatial distribution of radioactive label","The fraction of all proteins that are catalytically active"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Autoradiography maps where radiolabel is detected; assigning that label to a particular molecule requires knowledge of the precursor, experimental controls and complementary evidence."},{"id":"BI02-Q04","lesson":"BI02","question":"Which affects autoradiographic spatial resolution?","options":["Only paper baseline length","Only membrane cholesterol","Only rotor radius","Isotope emission and specimen thickness"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Emission range and specimen geometry influence localization."},{"id":"BI02-Q05","lesson":"BI02","question":"Which practice is essential when interpreting autoradiographic silver grains?","options":["Assume every grain is specific to the target","Omit unlabeled controls because film has no background","Treat grain size as an exact measurement of protein mass","Use suitable background controls and radiation precautions"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Silver grains can include background and nonspecific signal; controls are required for interpretation, and radioisotopes require appropriate radiation safety."},{"id":"BI03-Q01","lesson":"BI03","question":"The moving phase in chromatography is called the","options":["The phase that remains fixed in the system","Mobile phase","The solid support that holds the sample at the origin","The fraction recovered after centrifugation"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The mobile phase carries sample components."},{"id":"BI03-Q02","lesson":"BI03","question":"A solute binds more strongly to the stationary phase than another solute under the same column conditions. What usually happens?","options":["It elutes earlier because it moves faster","It remains permanently attached in all conditions","It elutes later because it spends more time retained","It has the same retention time regardless of affinity"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Greater affinity for the stationary phase usually slows migration and delays elution, all else equal."},{"id":"BI03-Q03","lesson":"BI03","question":"Partition chromatography relies mainly on","options":["Only differences in radioactive decay","Different distribution between phases","Centrifugal force alone","Protein synthesis speed"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Relative solubility between phases underlies partition."},{"id":"BI03-Q04","lesson":"BI03","question":"Which change can alter chromatographic retention?","options":["Mobile-phase composition","Changing centrifuge tube colour","Increasing film exposure only","Changing microscope objective only"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Solvent conditions influence phase interactions."},{"id":"BI03-Q05","lesson":"BI03","question":"Can early column elution alone identify a substance?","options":["Only if the column is transparent","Only if the solvent is water","No, standards and controls are needed","Yes, always"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Retention is condition-dependent and not unique identification."},{"id":"BI04-Q01","lesson":"BI04","question":"In paper chromatography the sample baseline should be","options":["Fully submerged","At the solvent front","Cut away before development","Above the solvent level"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Submerging spots can wash them into the reservoir."},{"id":"BI04-Q02","lesson":"BI04","question":"Solvent rises through paper mainly by","options":["Ultracentrifugation","Cell lysis","Radioactive emission","Capillary action"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Capillary action draws solvent through paper."},{"id":"BI04-Q03","lesson":"BI04","question":"When should the solvent front be marked?","options":["Before solvent is added to the chamber","After the solvent front has completely evaporated","Only after the chromatogram is stained","Immediately after removing the paper"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The front can become difficult to locate as solvent evaporates."},{"id":"BI04-Q04","lesson":"BI04","question":"Why do mixture components travel different distances?","options":["Identical phase affinities","Different interactions with two phases","Same visible colour","Same molecular mass always"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Different partitioning or adsorption leads to separation."},{"id":"BI04-Q05","lesson":"BI04","question":"A spot matching a standard’s position proves","options":["Absolute chemical identity","All compounds have the same Rf","Similarity under the tested conditions only","The sample is pure"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Co-migration alone is not definitive identification."},{"id":"BI05-Q01","lesson":"BI05","question":"Rf is calculated as","options":["Solvent-front distance divided by spot distance","Absorbance divided by wavelength","Spot distance times rotor speed","Spot distance divided by solvent-front distance"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Both distances start at the same baseline."},{"id":"BI05-Q02","lesson":"BI05","question":"In the lecture TLC example, a spot travels 1.4 cm while the solvent front travels 7.7 cm. What is its Rf?","options":["0.18","0.53","0.78","5.50"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Rf = 1.4/7.7 = 0.1818, approximately 0.18. Both distances are measured from the origin/baseline."},{"id":"BI05-Q03","lesson":"BI05","question":"In the slide’s column example, solute B emerges first because it","options":["Is less strongly retained than A","Is permanently bound to the column","Has a larger radioactive signal","Is always heavier than A"],"letter":"A","explanation":"B moves faster through the column."},{"id":"BI05-Q04","lesson":"BI05","question":"The lecture TLC plate has a 7.7 cm solvent front and a spot 6.0 cm from the origin. What is the Rf of that spot?","options":["0.53","0.78","1.28","0.18"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Rf = 6.0/7.7 = 0.7792, approximately 0.78, matching the second worked value on the lecture slide."},{"id":"BI05-Q05","lesson":"BI05","question":"A third spot on the lecture TLC plate travels 4.1 cm from the baseline, with the solvent front at 7.7 cm. Which Rf matches the worked solution?","options":["0.18","0.78","0.53","1.88"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Rf = 4.1/7.7 = 0.5325, approximately 0.53. Rf is dimensionless."},{"id":"MI-Q001","lesson":"lesson_01","question":"Which property lets a microscope distinguish two close points?","options":["Magnification","Resolution","Contrast","Depth of field"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Resolution is the ability to separate closely spaced points."},{"id":"MI-Q002","lesson":"lesson_02","question":"A simple microscope forms a magnified image primarily using","options":["Two separate objective lenses","A concave mirror alone","A single converging lens","A scanning electron beam"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A simple microscope uses a single lens or lens system as a magnifier."},{"id":"MI-Q003","lesson":"lesson_03","question":"Chromatic aberration arises because","options":["Spherical aberration from peripheral rays","Incorrect condenser centering","Reduced objective numerical aperture","Different wavelengths are refracted differently"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Wavelength-dependent refraction causes different colours to focus differently."},{"id":"MI-Q004","lesson":"lesson_04","question":"In a compound microscope, the objective lens first","options":["Forms a magnified intermediate image","Forms only the final virtual image","Produces a reduced intermediate image","Projects an electron image onto a screen"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The objective produces the primary magnified image, enlarged further by the eyepiece."},{"id":"MI-Q005","lesson":"lesson_05","question":"Parfocal objectives are designed so that","options":["Have identical working distances","Have identical resolving powers","The specimen stays nearly in focus after changing objectives","Require identical numerical apertures"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Parfocality reduces the refocusing required when changing objectives."},{"id":"MI-Q006","lesson":"lesson_06","question":"Which lens property is central to microscope resolution?","options":["Eyepiece magnification alone","Field diaphragm diameter alone","Objective barrel length alone","Numerical aperture"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Numerical aperture determines the angular range of light collected."},{"id":"MI-Q007","lesson":"lesson_07","question":"In dark-field microscopy, the specimen appears bright because","options":["Unscattered central rays enter the objective","Scattered light reaches the objective against a dark field","Light is emitted by a fluorophore","Only transmitted direct light reaches the objective"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Dark-field optics block direct rays and use specimen-scattered light."},{"id":"MI-Q008","lesson":"lesson_08","question":"Phase-contrast microscopy is especially useful for","options":["Unstained, transparent living cells","Fixed, metal-coated specimens","Thick, opaque mineral sections","Cells viewed solely by electron transmission"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Phase differences produced by transparent structures become visible intensity differences."},{"id":"MI-Q009","lesson":"lesson_09","question":"Ultraviolet microscopy can offer better theoretical resolution than visible-light microscopy because","options":["Greater eyepiece magnification","Greater specimen thickness","Ultraviolet light has a shorter wavelength","A longer illumination wavelength"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Shorter wavelengths can improve the diffraction-limited resolving distance."},{"id":"MI-Q010","lesson":"lesson_10","question":"Immunofluorescence localizes a target using","options":["Fluorophore-labelled lipids with no targeting","Electron-dense heavy-metal salts","Silver-halide photographic film only","Antibodies coupled directly or indirectly to fluorescent labels"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Fluorescent antibody-based detection visualizes specific antigens."},{"id":"MI-Q011","lesson":"lesson_11","question":"Transmission electron microscopy is best suited to studying","options":["Three-dimensional external surface topography","Internal ultrastructure in thin specimens","Direct observation of living cells in air","Fluorescent antigen localization"],"letter":"B","explanation":"TEM images electrons transmitted through thin specimens."},{"id":"MI-Q012","lesson":"lesson_12","question":"Scanning electron microscopy is especially useful for","options":["Surface morphology","Internal detail in ultrathin sections","Fluorescent antigen localization","Live-cell phase-contrast imaging"],"letter":"A","explanation":"SEM images surface-related signals."},{"id":"MI-Q013","lesson":"lesson_13","question":"Why is specimen preparation often necessary before microscopy?","options":["Increase objective numerical aperture","Convert all organelles to equal density","To preserve structures and create suitable contrast or thickness","Eliminate all biological variability"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Preparation supports preservation, imaging compatibility and contrast."},{"id":"MI-Q014","lesson":"lesson_14","question":"A primary purpose of chemical fixation is to","options":["Remove all water before paraffin infiltration","Produce permanent fluorescence in every cell","Remove every lipid from the sample","Preserve cellular structure and reduce autolysis"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Fixatives stabilize cellular constituents and limit post-collection change."},{"id":"MI-Q015","lesson":"lesson_15","question":"During routine paraffin histology, dehydration primarily removes","options":["Paraffin wax from tissue","Water from the tissue","Heavy-metal stains from tissue","All nucleic acids from tissue"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Water is replaced through graded alcohols before clearing and wax infiltration."},{"id":"MI-Q016","lesson":"lesson_16","question":"A microtome is used to","options":["Cut thin tissue sections","Generate electron beams for imaging","Measure the absorbance of stains","Illuminate the condenser"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Microtomes cut thin sections for microscopy."},{"id":"MI-Q017","lesson":"lesson_17","question":"Haematoxylin in routine H&E staining typically colours nuclei","options":["Pink to red","Brown to black","Blue to purple","Bright green"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Haematoxylin stains basophilic structures, including nuclei, blue-purple."},{"id":"MI-Q018","lesson":"lesson_18","question":"In conventional TEM negative staining, which region appears relatively electron-dense?","options":["The background surrounding the particle","The particle interior only, with a bright background","Only the specimen holder, not the sample","The vacuum outside the microscope column"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Electron-dense heavy-metal stain accumulates around the specimen, making the background dark while the particle is comparatively light."}]};
